1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,562
Chapter 7: Embryogenesis and Fetal Development.

3
00:00:06,562 --> 00:00:23,137
Gestational Age Embryonic Development Fetal Development And Physiology Energy And Nutrition Placental Role In Embryofetal Development References Contemporary obstetrics incorporates physiology and pathophysiology of the fetus, and its development and environment.

4
00:00:23,137 --> 00:00:29,875
As a result, the fetus is considered a patient and is given the same meticulous care provided for the mother.

5
00:00:29,875 --> 00:00:38,287
Section 6 is dedicated to the fetal patient, however, virtually every aspect of obstetrics can affect the developing fetus.

6
00:00:38,287 --> 00:00:40,375
Gestational Age.

7
00:00:40,375 --> 00:00:44,825
Several terms define pregnancy duration and thus fetal age .

8
00:00:44,825 --> 00:00:54,700
Gestational age or menstrual age is the time elapsed since the first day of the last menstrual period (LMP), a time that actually precedes conception.

9
00:00:54,700 --> 00:01:06,100
This starting time, which is usually approximately 2 weeks before ovulation and fertilization and nearly 3 weeks before blastocyst implantation, has traditionally been used.

10
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:13,812
Embryologists describe embryofetal development in ovulation age, or the time in days or weeks from ovulation.

11
00:01:13,812 --> 00:01:19,787
Another term is postconceptional age, which is nearly identical to ovulation age.

12
00:01:19,787 --> 00:01:22,000
Figure 7-1.

13
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,787
Terminology used to describe pregnancy duration.

14
00:01:25,787 --> 00:01:37,925
Until recently, clinicians customarily calculated menstrual age, and with this, term pregnancy averages 280 days or 40 weeks between the first day of the LMP and birth.

15
00:01:37,925 --> 00:01:41,850
This corresponds to 9 and 1/3 calendar months.

16
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:48,962
However, menstrual cycle length variability among women renders many of these calculations inaccurate.

17
00:01:48,962 --> 00:01:57,775
This realization, combined with the frequent use of first-trimester sonography, has led to more accurate gestational age determination .

18
00:01:57,775 --> 00:02:02,762
Much of this change stems from the accuracy of early sonographic measurement.

19
00:02:02,762 --> 00:02:16,950
As a result, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, the American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine, and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2019) together recommend the following: 1.

20
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:23,475
First-trimester sonography is the most accurate method to establish or reaffirm gestational age.

21
00:02:23,475 --> 00:02:31,362
2. In conceptions achieved with in vitro fertilization (IVF), the embryo age and egg transfer date are used.

22
00:02:31,362 --> 00:02:44,525
3. If available, the gestational ages calculated from the LMP and from firsttrimester sonography are compared, and the estimated date of confinement (EDC) is recorded and discussed with the patient.

23
00:02:44,525 --> 00:02:51,462
4. The best obstetrical estimate of gestational age at delivery is recorded on the birth certificate.

24
00:02:51,462 --> 00:02:58,350
The embryofetal crown-rump length in the first trimester is accurate ± 5 to 7 days.

25
00:02:58,350 --> 00:03:09,850
Thus, if sonographic gestational age differs by more than 5 days prior to 9 weeks’ gestation, or by more than 7 days later in the first trimester, the EDC is changed.

26
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:17,850
These and discrepant values in the second and third trimester are discussed further in Chapter 14 (p. 248).

27
00:03:17,850 --> 00:03:20,075
# Naegele Rule.

28
00:03:20,075 --> 00:03:29,250
An EDC based on the LMP can be quickly estimated as follows: add 7 days to the first day of the LMP and subtract 3 months.

29
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:42,100
For example, if the first day of the LMP was October 5, the due date is 10–05 minus 3 (months) plus 7 (days) = 7–12, or July 12 of the following year.

30
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:45,612
This calculation has been termed the Naegele rule.

31
00:03:45,612 --> 00:03:52,075
The period of gestation can also be divided into three units of approximately 14 weeks each.

32
00:03:52,075 --> 00:03:56,500
These three trimesters are important obstetrical milestones.

33
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:09,650
In addition to estimating the EDC with either Naegele rule or “pregnancy wheels,” calculator tools in the electronic medical record and smartphone applications can provide a calculated EDC and gestational age.

34
00:04:09,650 --> 00:04:23,162
For example, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020) has developed a calculator application that incorporates sonographic criteria and the LMP or embryo transfer date .

35
00:04:23,162 --> 00:04:25,425
Embryonic Development.

36
00:04:25,425 --> 00:04:29,300
The complexity of embryofetal development is immense.

37
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:34,737
Figure 7-2 shows a developmental sequence of various organ systems.

38
00:04:34,737 --> 00:04:39,225
New information regarding organ development continues to accrue.

39
00:04:39,225 --> 00:04:48,487
For example, imaging techniques help to unravel the contributions of gene regulation and tissue interaction to eventual three-dimensional organ

40
00:04:48,652 --> 00:04:50,452
morphology .

41
00:04:50,402 --> 00:04:57,589
Others have described the sequence of gene activation that underlies cardiac development (p. 126).

42
00:04:57,589 --> 00:04:59,877
Figure 7-2.

43
00:04:59,877 --> 00:05:06,377
Embryofetal development according to gestational age determined by the first day of the last menses.

44
00:05:06,377 --> 00:05:08,627
Times are approximate.

45
00:05:08,627 --> 00:05:12,064
# Zygote and Blastocyst Development.

46
00:05:12,064 --> 00:05:20,889
During the first 2 weeks after ovulation and then fertilization, the zygote—or preembryo—progresses to the blastocyst stage.

47
00:05:20,889 --> 00:05:25,727
The blastocyst implants 6 or 7 days following fertilization.

48
00:05:25,727 --> 00:05:33,464
The 58-cell blastocyst differentiates into five cells—the inner cell mass, which develops into the embryo.

49
00:05:33,464 --> 00:05:37,727
The remaining 53 cells form placental trophoblast.

50
00:05:37,727 --> 00:05:46,202
Details of implantation and early development of the blastocyst and placenta are described in Chapter 5 (p. 86).

51
00:05:46,202 --> 00:05:48,852
# Embryonic Period.

52
00:05:48,852 --> 00:05:55,302
The conceptus is termed an embryo at the beginning of the third week after ovulation and fertilization.

53
00:05:55,302 --> 00:06:01,214
Primitive chorionic villi form, and this coincides with the expected day of menses.

54
00:06:01,214 --> 00:06:07,377
The embryonic period, during which time organogenesis takes place, lasts 6 weeks.

55
00:06:07,377 --> 00:06:12,264
It begins the third week from the LMP and continues through the eighth week.

56
00:06:12,264 --> 00:06:22,052
The embryonic disc is well defined, and most pregnancy tests that measure human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) become positive by this time.

57
00:06:22,052 --> 00:06:27,239
As shown in Figure 7-3, the body stalk is now differentiated.

58
00:06:27,239 --> 00:06:35,802
There are villous cores in which angioblastic chorionic mesoderm can be distinguished and a true intervillous space that contains maternal blood.

59
00:06:35,802 --> 00:06:38,127
Figure 7-3.

60
00:06:38,127 --> 00:06:40,427
Early human embryos.

61
00:06:40,427 --> 00:06:44,827
Ovulation ages: A. 19 days (presomite).

62
00:06:44,827 --> 00:06:48,227
B. 21 days (7 somites).

63
00:06:48,227 --> 00:06:51,889
C. 22 days (17 somites).

64
00:06:51,889 --> 00:06:55,489
After drawings and models in the Carnegie Institute.

65
00:06:55,489 --> 00:07:00,414
During the third week, fetal blood vessels in the chorionic villi appear.

66
00:07:00,414 --> 00:07:04,689
In the fourth week, a cardiovascular system has formed .

67
00:07:04,689 --> 00:07:12,089
Thereby, a true circulation is established both within the embryo and between the embryo and the chorionic villi.

68
00:07:12,089 --> 00:07:15,202
Partitioning of the primitive heart begins.

69
00:07:15,202 --> 00:07:21,714
Also in the fourth week, the neural plate forms, and it subsequently folds to form the neural tube.

70
00:07:21,714 --> 00:07:28,802
By the end of the fifth menstrual week, the chorionic sac measures approximately 1 cm in diameter.

71
00:07:28,802 --> 00:07:33,677
The embryo is 3 mm long and can be measured sonographically.

72
00:07:33,677 --> 00:07:41,939
Arm and leg buds have developed, and the amnion is beginning to ensheathe the body stalk, which thereafter becomes the umbilical cord.

73
00:07:41,939 --> 00:07:49,039
At the end of the sixth week, the embryo is approximately 9 mm long, and the neural tube has closed .

74
00:07:49,039 --> 00:07:53,427
Cardiac motion is almost always discernable sonographically .

75
00:07:53,427 --> 00:07:55,852
Figure 7-4.

76
00:07:55,852 --> 00:07:58,789
Three- to four-week-old embryos.

77
00:07:58,789 --> 00:08:07,077
A, B. Dorsal views of embryos during 22 to 23 days of development showing 8 and 12 somites, respectively.

78
00:08:07,077 --> 00:08:17,039
C–E. Lateral views of embryos during 24 to 28 days, showing 16, 27, and 33 somites, respectively.

79
00:08:17,039 --> 00:08:19,502
Figure 7-5.

80
00:08:19,502 --> 00:08:21,627
Embryo photographs.

81
00:08:21,627 --> 00:08:28,852
A. Dorsal view of an embryo at 24 to 26 days and corresponding to Figure 7-4C.

82
00:08:28,852 --> 00:08:35,327
B. Lateral view of an embryo at 28 days and corresponding to Figure 7-4D.

83
00:08:35,327 --> 00:08:43,939
C. Lateral view of embryofetus at 56 days, which marks the end of the embryonic period and the beginning of the fetal period.

84
00:08:43,939 --> 00:08:47,227
The liver is within the white, halo circle.

85
00:08:47,227 --> 00:08:52,702
From Werth B, Tsiaras A: From Conception to Birth: A Life Unfolds.

86
00:08:52,702 --> 00:08:56,102
New York, Doubleday, 2002.

87
00:08:56,102 --> 00:08:58,564
Figure 7-6.

88
00:08:58,564 --> 00:09:08,477
A. This image of an 8-week, 3-day embryo depicts measurement of the crown-rump length, which is 1.93 cm at this gestational age.

89
00:09:08,477 --> 00:09:15,677
B. Despite the early gestational age, M-mode imaging readily demonstrates embryonic cardiac activity.

90
00:09:15,677 --> 00:09:20,264
The heart rate in this image is 161 beats per minute.

91
00:09:20,264 --> 00:09:28,189
The cranial end of the neural tube closes by 38 days from the LMP, and the caudal end closes by 40 days.

92
00:09:28,189 --> 00:09:32,702
Thus, the neural tube has closed by the end of the sixth week.

93
00:09:32,702 --> 00:09:41,902
And by the end of the eighth week, the crown-rump length approximates 22 mm. Fingers and toes are present, and the arms bend at the elbows.

94
00:09:41,902 --> 00:09:48,464
The upper lip is complete, and the external ears form definitive elevations on either side of the head.

95
00:09:48,464 --> 00:09:58,627
Three-dimensional images and videos of human embryos from the Multi-Dimensional Human Embryo project can be seen at: embryo.soad.umich.edu/.

96
00:09:58,627 --> 00:10:01,502
Fetal Development And Physiology.

97
00:10:01,502 --> 00:10:03,902
# Fetal Period

98
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:05,585
Epochs.

99
00:10:05,535 --> 00:10:14,560
The transition from embryonic to fetal periods occurs at 7 weeks after fertilization, corresponding to 9 weeks after the LMP.

100
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:24,847
At this time, the fetus approximates 24 mm in length, most organ systems have developed, and the fetus enters a period of growth and maturation.

101
00:10:24,847 --> 00:10:28,610
These phases are outlined in Figure 7-2.

102
00:10:28,610 --> 00:10:34,472
12 Gestational Weeks The uterus usually is just palpable above the symphysis pubis.

103
00:10:34,472 --> 00:10:40,247
Fetal growth is rapid, and the fetal crown-rump length is 5 to 6 cm .

104
00:10:40,247 --> 00:10:47,272
Centers of ossification have appeared in most fetal bones, and the fingers and toes have become differentiated.

105
00:10:47,272 --> 00:10:51,647
Skin and nails develop, and scattered rudiments of hair appear.

106
00:10:51,647 --> 00:10:57,572
The external genitalia are beginning to show definitive signs of male or female gender.

107
00:10:57,572 --> 00:11:01,185
The fetus begins to make spontaneous movements.

108
00:11:01,185 --> 00:11:03,597
Figure 7-7.

109
00:11:03,597 --> 00:11:09,385
This image of a 12-week, 2-day embryo depicts measurement of the crown-rump length.

110
00:11:09,385 --> 00:11:14,960
The fetal profile, cranium, and a hand and foot also are visible in this image.

111
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:19,235
16 Gestational Weeks Fetal growth slows at this time.

112
00:11:19,235 --> 00:11:34,197
The crown-rump length is 12 cm, and the fetal weight approximates 150 g. Clinically, the sonographic crown-rump length is not measured beyond 13 weeks, which corresponds to approximately 8.4 cm.

113
00:11:34,197 --> 00:11:41,510
Instead, biparietal diameter, head circumference, abdominal circumference, and femur length are measured.

114
00:11:41,510 --> 00:11:47,547
Fetal weight in the second and third trimesters is estimated from a combination of these measurements .

115
00:11:47,547 --> 00:11:53,322
Eye movements begin at 16 to 18 weeks, coinciding with midbrain maturation.

116
00:11:53,322 --> 00:11:59,797
By 18 weeks in the female fetus, the uterus is formed and vaginal canalization begins.

117
00:11:59,797 --> 00:12:03,785
By 20 weeks in the male, testicles start to descend.

118
00:12:03,785 --> 00:12:09,560
20 Gestational Weeks This is the midpoint of pregnancy as estimated from the LMP.

119
00:12:09,560 --> 00:12:16,372
The fetus now weighs somewhat more than 300 g, and weight increases substantially in a linear manner.

120
00:12:16,372 --> 00:12:23,410
From this point onward, the fetus moves approximately every minute and is active 10 to 30 percent of the day .

121
00:12:23,410 --> 00:12:28,035
Brown fat forms, and the fetal skin becomes less transparent.

122
00:12:28,035 --> 00:12:33,097
Downy lanugo covers its entire body, and some scalp hair can be seen.

123
00:12:33,097 --> 00:12:41,285
Cochlear function develops between 22 and 25 weeks, and this maturation continues for 6 months after delivery.

124
00:12:41,285 --> 00:12:46,372
24 Gestational Weeks The fetus now weighs almost 700 g.

125
00:12:46,372 --> 00:12:51,272
The skin is characteristically wrinkled, and fat deposition begins.

126
00:12:51,272 --> 00:12:57,472
The head is still comparatively large, and eyebrows and eyelashes are usually recognizable.

127
00:12:57,472 --> 00:13:03,872
By 24 weeks, the secretory type II pneumocytes have initiated surfactant secretion .

128
00:13:03,872 --> 00:13:12,622
The canalicular period of lung development, during which the bronchi and bronchioles enlarge and alveolar ducts develop, is nearly completed.

129
00:13:12,622 --> 00:13:22,647
Despite this, a fetus born at this time will attempt to breathe, but many will die because the terminal sacs, required for gas exchange, have not yet formed.

130
00:13:22,647 --> 00:13:34,872
Although dependent on racial and ethnic factors, and as discussed in Chapter 45 (p. 785), the overall survival rate at 24 weeks barely exceeds 50 percent .

131
00:13:34,872 --> 00:13:37,997
By 26 weeks, the eyes open.

132
00:13:37,997 --> 00:13:43,672
Nociceptors are present over all the body, and the neural pain system is developed .

133
00:13:43,672 --> 00:13:48,535
The fetal liver and spleen are important early sites for hemopoiesis .

134
00:13:48,535 --> 00:13:57,447
28 Gestational Weeks The crown-rump length approximates 25 cm, and the fetus weighs about 1100 g.

135
00:13:57,447 --> 00:14:01,672
The thin skin is red and covered with vernix caseosa.

136
00:14:01,672 --> 00:14:05,647
The pupillary membrane has just disappeared from the eyes.

137
00:14:05,647 --> 00:14:09,185
Isolated eye blinking peaks at 28 weeks.

138
00:14:09,185 --> 00:14:13,510
The bone marrow now becomes the major site of hemopoiesis.

139
00:14:13,510 --> 00:14:21,397
The otherwise normal neonate born at this age has a 90-percent chance of survival without physical or neurological impairment.

140
00:14:21,397 --> 00:14:32,272
32 and 36 Gestational Weeks At 32 weeks, the fetus has attained a crown-rump length approximating 28 cm and a weight of about 1800 g.

141
00:14:32,272 --> 00:14:35,435
The skin surface is still red and wrinkled.

142
00:14:35,435 --> 00:14:45,397
In contrast, by 36 weeks, the fetal crown-rump length averages about 32 cm, and the weight approximates 2800 g .

143
00:14:45,397 --> 00:14:52,922
Because of subcutaneous fat deposition, the body is more rotund, and the previous wrinkled facies are now fuller.

144
00:14:52,922 --> 00:14:57,335
Normal fetuses have a nearly 100-percent survival rate.

145
00:14:57,335 --> 00:15:02,797
40 Gestational Weeks This is considered term, and the fetus is fully developed.

146
00:15:02,956 --> 00:15:13,918
The average crown-rump length measures about 36 cm, and the average weight approximates 3500 g. # Central Nervous System Development.

147
00:15:13,868 --> 00:15:23,268
Brain Development The cranial end of the neural tube closes by 38 days from the last menstrual period, and the caudal end closes by 40 days.

148
00:15:23,268 --> 00:15:30,968
Hence, folic acid supplementation to prevent neural-tube defects must be in place before this point to be efficacious .

149
00:15:30,968 --> 00:15:35,031
The walls of the neural tube form the brain and spinal cord.

150
00:15:35,031 --> 00:15:40,793
The lumen becomes the ventricular system of the brain and the central canal of the spinal cord.

151
00:15:40,793 --> 00:15:46,618
During the sixth week, the cranial end of the neural tube forms three primary vesicles.

152
00:15:46,618 --> 00:16:01,993
In the seventh week, five secondary vesicles develop: the telencephalon—future cerebral hemispheres; diencephalon—thalami; mesencephalon—midbrain; metencephalon—pons and cerebellum; and myelencephalon—medulla.

153
00:16:01,993 --> 00:16:10,593
This is, in part, controlled by Hox genes, and defects result in abnormal signaling that leads to neuropathic anomalies .

154
00:16:10,593 --> 00:16:16,006
Meanwhile, flexures develop and fold the brain into its typical configuration.

155
00:16:16,006 --> 00:16:22,393
The end of the embryonic period signifies completion of primary and secondary neutralization.

156
00:16:22,393 --> 00:16:27,031
At 3 to 4 months’ gestation, neuronal proliferation peaks.

157
00:16:27,031 --> 00:16:36,956
As expected, disorders in this cerebral development phase profoundly worsen function (Ortega, 2017; Volpe, 2018).

158
00:16:36,956 --> 00:16:40,231
One example is Zika virus infection .

159
00:16:40,231 --> 00:16:45,931
Neuronal migration occurs almost simultaneously and peaks at 3 to 5 months.

160
00:16:45,931 --> 00:16:55,956
This process is characterized by movement of millions of neuronal cells from their ventricular and subventricular zones to areas of the brain in which they reside for life .

161
00:16:55,956 --> 00:17:01,031
Upregulation of gene expression for neuronal migration has been described .

162
00:17:01,031 --> 00:17:10,193
Noninvasive methods to study fetal neurodevelopment also have been reported (Goetzl, 2016; Wang, 2015).

163
00:17:10,193 --> 00:17:12,431
Figure 7-8.

164
00:17:12,431 --> 00:17:17,856
Neuronal proliferation and migration are complete at 20 to 24 weeks.

165
00:17:17,856 --> 00:17:28,493
During the second half of gestation, organizational events proceed with gyral formation and proliferation, differentiation, and migration of cellular elements.

166
00:17:28,493 --> 00:17:31,656
Approximate gestational ages are shown.

167
00:17:31,656 --> 00:17:33,843
A. 20 weeks.

168
00:17:33,843 --> 00:17:36,231
B. 35 weeks.

169
00:17:36,231 --> 00:17:38,406
C. 40 weeks.

170
00:17:38,406 --> 00:17:43,306
As gestation progresses, the fetal brain appearance steadily changes.

171
00:17:43,306 --> 00:17:48,493
Thus, it is possible to identify fetal age from its external appearance .

172
00:17:48,493 --> 00:17:55,681
Neuronal proliferation and migration proceed along with gyral growth and maturation (see Fig. 7-8).

173
00:17:55,681 --> 00:18:09,293
Sequential maturation studies using magnetic resonance (MR) imaging have characterized the developing fetal brain (Dubois, 2014; Meng, 2012; Wang, 2015).

174
00:18:09,293 --> 00:18:19,118
Myelination of the ventral roots of the cerebrospinal nerves and brainstem begins at approximately 6 months, but most myelination progresses after birth.

175
00:18:19,118 --> 00:18:27,456
This lack of myelin and incomplete skull ossification permit fetal brain structure to be seen sonographically throughout gestation.

176
00:18:27,456 --> 00:18:35,643
Spinal Cord Whereas the superior two thirds of the neural tube give rise to the brain, the inferior third forms the spinal cord.

177
00:18:35,643 --> 00:18:43,993
In the embryo, the spinal cord extends along the entire vertebral column length, but after that it lags behind vertebral growth.

178
00:18:43,993 --> 00:18:50,418
Ossification of the entire sacrum is visible sonographically by approximately 21 weeks .

179
00:18:50,418 --> 00:18:57,618
By 24 weeks, the spinal cord extends to S1, at birth to L3, and in the adult to L1.

180
00:18:57,618 --> 00:19:03,493
Spinal cord myelination begins at midgestation and continues through the first year of life.

181
00:19:03,493 --> 00:19:09,931
Synaptic function is sufficiently developed by the eighth week to demonstrate flexion of the neck and trunk.

182
00:19:09,931 --> 00:19:16,081
During the third trimester, integration of nervous and muscular function proceeds rapidly .

183
00:19:16,081 --> 00:19:19,081
# Cardiovascular System.

184
00:19:19,081 --> 00:19:22,656
The embryology of the heart is highly complex.

185
00:19:22,656 --> 00:19:36,881
At its earliest stages of formation, the fetal heart undergoes molecular programming, and more than a hundred genes and molecular factors are integral to its morphogenesis (Kathiriya, 2015; Moore, 2020).

186
00:19:36,881 --> 00:19:44,781
These molecular factors include the hypoxia-inducible factor—HIF and homeobox (HOX)—family.

187
00:19:44,781 --> 00:19:56,006
To summarize its embryology, the straight cardiac tube is formed by the 23rd day during an intricate morphogenetic sequence, during which each segment arises at a unique time.

188
00:19:56,006 --> 00:19:58,268
Between 4 and 7

189
00:19:58,420 --> 00:20:08,732
weeks the heart undergoes extensive growth and morphological modification, leading to the formation of a partially septated four-chambered heart with a set of primitive valves .

190
00:20:08,682 --> 00:20:13,682
The valves develop, and the aortic arch forms by vasculogenesis.

191
00:20:13,682 --> 00:20:17,332
Chapter 8 of Hurst’s The Heart has a full description .

192
00:20:17,332 --> 00:20:23,095
Late in fetal life, coronary angiogenesis vascularizes the myocardium .

193
00:20:23,095 --> 00:20:31,807
Fetal Circulation This unique circulation is substantially different from that of the adult and functions until birth, when it changes dramatically.

194
00:20:31,807 --> 00:20:39,045
For example, fetal blood is oxygenated by the placenta and does not need to enter the pulmonary vasculature.

195
00:20:39,045 --> 00:20:43,782
Thus, most of the right ventricular output bypasses the lungs.

196
00:20:43,782 --> 00:20:48,870
In addition, the fetal heart chambers work in parallel, not in series.

197
00:20:48,870 --> 00:20:57,757
This effectively supplies the brain and heart, compared with the rest of the body, with more highly oxygenated blood from the dominant right ventricle.

198
00:20:57,757 --> 00:21:05,982
Oxygen and nutrient materials required for fetal growth and maturation are delivered from the placenta by the single umbilical vein .

199
00:21:05,982 --> 00:21:10,607
The vein then divides into the ductus venosus and the portal sinus.

200
00:21:10,607 --> 00:21:18,632
The ductus venosus is the major branch of the umbilical vein and traverses the liver to enter the inferior vena cava directly.

201
00:21:18,632 --> 00:21:26,057
Because it does not supply oxygen to the intervening tissues, it carries well-oxygenated blood directly to the heart.

202
00:21:26,057 --> 00:21:34,870
In contrast, the portal sinus carries blood to the hepatic veins primarily on the left side of the liver, and oxygen is extracted.

203
00:21:34,870 --> 00:21:45,332
The relatively deoxygenated blood from the liver then flows back into the inferior vena cava, which also receives more deoxygenated blood returning from the lower body.

204
00:21:45,332 --> 00:22:00,682
Blood flowing to the fetal heart from the inferior vena cava, therefore, consists of an admixture of arterial-like blood that passes directly through the ductus venosus and less well-oxygenated blood that returns from most of the veins below the level of the diaphragm.

205
00:22:00,682 --> 00:22:08,157
The oxygen content of blood delivered to the heart from the inferior vena cava is thus lower than that leaving the placenta.

206
00:22:08,157 --> 00:22:10,520
Figure 7-9.

207
00:22:10,520 --> 00:22:14,557
The intricate nature of the fetal circulation is evident.

208
00:22:14,557 --> 00:22:20,957
The degree of blood oxygenation in various vessels differs appreciably from that in the postnatal state.

209
00:22:20,957 --> 00:22:32,120
aa = arteries; LA = left atrium; LV = left ventricle; RA = right atrium; RV = right ventricle; v = vein.

210
00:22:32,120 --> 00:22:40,795
Because the ventricles of the fetal heart work in parallel, this allows the right ventricle to account for two thirds of the total cardiac output.

211
00:22:40,795 --> 00:22:51,182
Welloxygenated blood enters the left ventricle, which supplies the heart and brain, and less oxygenated blood enters the right ventricle, which supplies the rest of the body.

212
00:22:51,182 --> 00:23:02,195
Congenital cardiac defects may contribute to dysregulated brain development or placenta dysfunction (Fantasia, 2018; Lauridsen, 2017).

213
00:23:02,195 --> 00:23:13,932
These two separate circulations are maintained by right-atrium anatomy, which effectively directs entering blood to either the left atrium or the right ventricle, depending on its oxygen content.

214
00:23:13,932 --> 00:23:21,407
This separation of blood according to its oxygen content is aided by the pattern of blood flow in the inferior vena cava.

215
00:23:21,407 --> 00:23:31,470
The well-oxygenated blood tends to course along the dorsomedial aspect of the inferior vena cava and the less oxygenated blood flows along the lateral vessel wall.

216
00:23:31,470 --> 00:23:35,245
This aids their shunting into opposite sides of the heart.

217
00:23:35,245 --> 00:23:50,357
Once this blood enters the right atrium, the configuration of the upper interatrial septum—the crista dividens—preferentially shunts the well-oxygenated blood from the medial side of the inferior vena cava through the foramen ovale into the left heart.

218
00:23:50,357 --> 00:23:53,532
Here, it is directed to the heart and brain .

219
00:23:53,532 --> 00:24:02,532
After these tissues extract needed oxygen, the resulting less oxygenated blood returns to the right atrium through the superior vena cava.

220
00:24:02,532 --> 00:24:08,120
Blood flow velocity in the superior vena cava rises from 20 weeks until term .

221
00:24:08,120 --> 00:24:18,257
The less oxygenated blood coursing along the lateral wall of the inferior vena cava enters the right atrium and is deflected through the tricuspid valve to the right ventricle.

222
00:24:18,257 --> 00:24:23,370
The superior vena cava courses inferiorly and anteriorly as

223
00:24:23,524 --> 00:24:32,986
it enters the right atrium, ensuring that less well-oxygenated blood returning from the brain and upper body also will be shunted directly to the right ventricle.

224
00:24:32,936 --> 00:24:43,449
Similarly, the ostium of the coronary sinus lies just superior to the tricuspid valve so that less oxygenated blood from the heart also returns to the right ventricle.

225
00:24:43,449 --> 00:24:52,586
As a result of this blood flow pattern, blood in the right ventricle is 15 to 20 percent less saturated with oxygen than blood in the left ventricle.

226
00:24:52,586 --> 00:24:59,911
Almost 90 percent of blood exiting the right ventricle is shunted through the ductus arteriosus to the descending aorta.

227
00:24:59,911 --> 00:25:13,136
High pulmonary vascular resistance and comparatively lower resistance in the ductus arteriosus and the umbilical–placental vasculature ensure that only about 8 percent of right ventricular output goes to the lungs .

228
00:25:13,136 --> 00:25:18,849
Thus, one third of the blood passing through the ductus arteriosus is delivered to the body.

229
00:25:18,849 --> 00:25:25,161
The remaining right ventricular output returns to the placenta through the two hypogastric arteries.

230
00:25:25,161 --> 00:25:33,811
These two arteries course from the level of the bladder along the abdominal wall to the umbilical ring and into the cord as the umbilical arteries.

231
00:25:33,811 --> 00:25:40,936
In the placenta, this blood picks up oxygen and other nutrients and is recirculated to the umbilical vein.

232
00:25:40,936 --> 00:25:51,474
Circulatory Changes at Birth After birth, the umbilical vessels, ductus arteriosus, foramen ovale, and ductus venosus normally constrict or collapse.

233
00:25:51,474 --> 00:26:04,536
With the functional closure of the ductus arteriosus and the expansion of the lungs, blood leaving the right ventricle preferentially enters the pulmonary vasculature to become oxygenated before it returns to the left heart .

234
00:26:04,536 --> 00:26:12,224
Virtually instantaneously, the ventricles, which had worked in parallel in fetal life, now effectively work in series.

235
00:26:12,224 --> 00:26:19,961
The more distal portions of the hypogastric arteries undergo atrophy and obliteration within 3 to 4 days after birth.

236
00:26:19,961 --> 00:26:27,924
These become the umbilical ligaments, whereas the intraabdominal remnants of the umbilical vein form the ligamentum teres.

237
00:26:27,924 --> 00:26:35,661
The ductus venosus constricts by 10 to 96 hours after birth and is anatomically closed by 2 to 3 weeks.

238
00:26:35,661 --> 00:26:39,161
This ultimately forms the ligamentum venosum .

239
00:26:39,161 --> 00:26:55,936
Fetoplacental Blood Volume Although precise measurements of human fetoplacental blood volume are lacking, Usher and associates (1963) reported values in term normal newborns to average 78 mL/kg when immediate cord clamping was conducted.

240
00:26:55,936 --> 00:27:06,911
Gruenwald (1967) found that the fetal blood volume contained in the placenta after prompt cord clamping averaged 45 mL/kg of fetal weight.

241
00:27:06,911 --> 00:27:14,886
Thus, fetoplacental blood volume at term is approximately 125 mL/kg of fetal weight.

242
00:27:14,886 --> 00:27:23,299
This is important when assessing the magnitude of fetomaternal hemorrhage, which is discussed in Chapter 18 (p. 358).

243
00:27:23,299 --> 00:27:25,799
# Hemopoiesis.

244
00:27:25,799 --> 00:27:33,411
Embryo hemopoiesis begins in the yolk sac and endothelium, followed by the liver, and then spleen and bone marrow .

245
00:27:33,411 --> 00:27:38,699
Transitions are intricate and involve several genes and protein complexes .

246
00:27:38,699 --> 00:27:50,149
Both myeloid and erythroid cells are continually produced by progenitors that derive from hemopoietic stem cells (Fanni, 2018; Heinig, 2015).

247
00:27:50,149 --> 00:27:55,999
The first erythrocytes released into the fetal circulation are nucleated and macrocytic.

248
00:27:55,999 --> 00:28:04,374
The mean cell volume is at least 180 fL in the embryo and decreases to 105 to 115 fL at term.

249
00:28:04,374 --> 00:28:08,724
Normal adult volume ranges from 80 to 95 fL.

250
00:28:08,724 --> 00:28:18,486
The erythrocytes of aneuploid fetuses generally do not undergo this maturation and maintain high mean cell volumes, which average 130 fL .

251
00:28:18,486 --> 00:28:25,436
As fetal development progresses, more and more circulating erythrocytes are smaller and nonnucleated.

252
00:28:25,436 --> 00:28:33,024
With fetal growth, both the blood volume in the common fetoplacental circulation and hemoglobin concentration increase.

253
00:28:33,024 --> 00:28:39,511
As shown in Table 7-1, fetal hemoglobin concentrations rise across pregnancy.

254
00:28:39,511 --> 00:28:50,211
For clinical purposes, the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2015) recommends a cutoff fetal hematocrit value of 30 percent to define anemia.

255
00:28:50,211 --> 00:28:52,511
Table 7-1.

256
00:28:52,511 --> 00:29:04,399
Fetal Hemoglobin Concentrations Across Pregnancy Because of their large size, fetal erythrocytes have a short life span, which progressively lengthens to approximately 90 days at term .

257
00:29:04,564 --> 00:29:08,989
As a consequence, red blood cell concentrations rise.

258
00:29:08,939 --> 00:29:15,601
Reticulocytes are initially present at high levels but decrease to 4 to 5 percent of the total at term.

259
00:29:15,601 --> 00:29:20,801
Fetal erythrocytes differ structurally and metabolically from those in the adult .

260
00:29:20,801 --> 00:29:26,039
They are more deformable, which serves to offset their higher volume and viscosity.

261
00:29:26,039 --> 00:29:30,926
They also contain several enzymes with appreciably different activities.

262
00:29:30,926 --> 00:29:39,089
Erythropoiesis is controlled primarily by fetal erythropoietin because maternal erythropoietin does not cross the placenta.

263
00:29:39,089 --> 00:29:49,939
Fetal hormone production is influenced by testosterone, estrogen, prostaglandins, thyroid hormone, lipoproteins, and importantly, by fetal hypoxia .

264
00:29:49,939 --> 00:29:54,151
Serum erythropoietin levels rise with fetal maturity.

265
00:29:54,151 --> 00:29:59,314
The fetal liver is an important source until renal production begins near term.

266
00:29:59,314 --> 00:30:08,076
The erythropoietin concentration in amnionic fluid correlates closely with that in umbilical venous blood obtained by cordocentesis.

267
00:30:08,076 --> 00:30:13,801
After birth, erythropoietin normally may not be detectable for up to 3 months.

268
00:30:13,801 --> 00:30:21,789
In contrast, platelet production reaches stable levels by midpregnancy, although there is some variation across gestation .

269
00:30:21,789 --> 00:30:30,164
The fetal and neonatal platelet count is subject to various agents, which are discussed in Chapter 18 (p. 359).

270
00:30:30,164 --> 00:30:32,451
Figure 7-10.

271
00:30:32,451 --> 00:30:36,876
Platelet counts by gestational age obtained the first day of life.

272
00:30:36,876 --> 00:30:41,189
Mean values and 5th and 95th percentiles are shown.

273
00:30:41,189 --> 00:30:56,964
Data from Christensen RD, Henry E, Antonio DV: Thrombocytosis and thrombocytopenia in the NICU: incidence, mechanisms and treatments, J Matern Fetal Neonatal Med 25 Suppl 4:15, 2012.

274
00:30:56,964 --> 00:31:06,289
Fetal Hemoglobin This tetrameric protein is composed of two copies of two different peptide chains, which determine the type of hemoglobin produced .

275
00:31:06,289 --> 00:31:11,014
Normal adult hemoglobin A1 is made of α and β chains.

276
00:31:11,014 --> 00:31:17,226
During embryonic and fetal life, various α and β chain precursors are produced.

277
00:31:17,226 --> 00:31:22,489
This results in the serial production of several different embryonic hemoglobins.

278
00:31:22,489 --> 00:31:27,776
At least 17 genetic loci potentially regulate erythropoiesis .

279
00:31:27,776 --> 00:31:34,889
Genes for β-type chains are on chromosome 11, and those for α-type chains on chromosome 16.

280
00:31:34,889 --> 00:31:46,051
Each of these genes is turned on and then off during fetal life, until α and β genes, which direct the production of adult hemoglobin A1, are permanently activated.

281
00:31:46,051 --> 00:31:48,439
Figure 7-11.

282
00:31:48,439 --> 00:31:52,139
Hemoglobin types transition throughout pregnancy.

283
00:31:52,139 --> 00:31:56,851
Alpha (α) and zeta (ζ) globin chains are interchangeable.

284
00:31:56,851 --> 00:32:06,026
The beta (β) globin chain can be replaced by epsilon (ε), gamma (γ), or delta ( δ) globin chains.

285
00:32:06,026 --> 00:32:15,301
In embryonic life (stage I), ζ and ε globin chain production is gradually replaced by α and γ globin chain production.

286
00:32:15,301 --> 00:32:19,676
Hemoglobin F is the main type in fetal life (stage II).

287
00:32:19,676 --> 00:32:24,076
After birth (stage III), HbA1 predominates.

288
00:32:24,076 --> 00:32:30,639
The timing of production of each of these early hemoglobins corresponds to the site of hemoglobin production.

289
00:32:30,639 --> 00:32:38,051
Fetal blood is first produced in the yolk sac, where hemoglobins Gower 1, Gower 2, and Portland are made.

290
00:32:38,051 --> 00:32:43,501
Erythropoiesis then moves to the liver, where fetal hemoglobin F is produced.

291
00:32:43,501 --> 00:32:54,789
When hemopoiesis finally moves to the bone marrow, adult-type hemoglobin A1 appears in fetal red blood cells and is present in progressively greater amounts as the fetus matures .

292
00:32:54,789 --> 00:33:00,264
The final adult version of the α chain is produced exclusively by 6 weeks.

293
00:33:00,264 --> 00:33:07,726
After this, there are no functional alternative versions, and the gene for the zeta (ζ) globin chain is down-regulated.

294
00:33:07,726 --> 00:33:16,714
If the gene coding the α globin chain undergoes mutation, no alternate α-type chain can be substituted to form functional hemoglobin.

295
00:33:16,714 --> 00:33:24,764
In contrast, at least two versions of the β chain—δ and γ—remain in production throughout fetal life and beyond.

296
00:33:24,764 --> 00:33:42,551
With a mutation in the gene coding the β globin chain, these two other versions of the β chain often continue to be produced, resulting in either hemoglobin A2 (α2δ2) or hemoglobin F (α2γ2), which substitute for the abnormal or missing hemoglobin A1.

297
00:33:42,551 --> 00:33:50,264
Genes are turned off by methylation of their control region, which is discussed in Chapter 13 (p. 322).

298
00:33:50,264 --> 00:33:54,251
In some situations, methylation does not occur.

299
00:33:54,251 --> 00:33:57,914
For example, in newborns of diabetic women,

300
00:33:58,084 --> 00:34:02,834
hemoglobin F may persist due to hypomethylation of the γ gene .

301
00:34:02,784 --> 00:34:11,271
With sickle-cell anemia, the γ gene remains unmethylated, and large quantities of fetal hemoglobin continue to be produced.

302
00:34:11,271 --> 00:34:27,321
As discussed in Chapter 59 (p. 1053), elevated hemoglobin F levels are associated with fewer sickle-cell disease symptoms, and pharmacological modification of these levels by hemoglobin F–inducing drugs is one treatment approach .

303
00:34:27,321 --> 00:34:30,771
Hemoglobins A and F function differently.

304
00:34:30,771 --> 00:34:45,146
At any given oxygen tension and at identical pH, fetal erythrocytes that contain mostly hemoglobin F bind more oxygen than do those that contain nearly all hemoglobin A (Fig. 50-2, p. 886).

305
00:34:45,146 --> 00:35:02,909
This is because hemoglobin A binds 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (2,3DPG) more avidly than does hemoglobin F. Remember that 2,3-DPG is an intraerythrocyte phosphate, and hemoglobin has a reciprocal affinity for 2,3DPG and oxygen .

306
00:35:02,909 --> 00:35:10,809
Thus, hemoglobin A’s greater 2,3-DPG binding lowers its oxygen affinity compared with hemoglobin F.

307
00:35:10,809 --> 00:35:24,084
Moreover, during pregnancy, maternal 2,3-DPG levels are greater, and because fetal erythrocytes have lower concentrations of 2,3-DPG, fetal cells have increased oxygen affinity.

308
00:35:24,084 --> 00:35:30,584
The amount of hemoglobin F in fetal erythrocytes begins to decrease in the last weeks of pregnancy.

309
00:35:30,584 --> 00:35:36,446
At term, approximately three fourths of total hemoglobin levels are hemoglobin F.

310
00:35:36,446 --> 00:35:46,546
During the first 6 to 12 months of life, the hemoglobin F proportion continues to decline and eventually reaches the low levels found in adult erythrocytes .

311
00:35:46,546 --> 00:35:54,509
Coagulation Factors With the exception of fibrinogen, other hemostatic proteins do not exist in embryonic forms.

312
00:35:54,509 --> 00:36:02,696
The fetus starts producing normal, adult-type procoagulant, fibrinolytic, and anticoagulant proteins by 12 weeks.

313
00:36:02,696 --> 00:36:10,834
Because they do not cross the placenta, their concentrations at birth are markedly below the levels that develop within a few weeks of life .

314
00:36:10,834 --> 00:36:24,134
In normal neonates, the levels of factors II, VII, IX, X, and XI, and of protein S, protein C, antithrombin, and plasminogen, all approximate 50 percent of adult levels.

315
00:36:24,134 --> 00:36:31,384
In contrast, levels of factors V, VIII, XIII, and fibrinogen are closer to adult values .

316
00:36:31,384 --> 00:36:36,771
Maternal vitamin K deficiency has been associated with fetal cerebral hemorrhage .

317
00:36:36,771 --> 00:36:46,284
Without prophylactic treatment, the levels of vitamin K–dependent coagulation factors usually drop even further during the first few days after birth.

318
00:36:46,284 --> 00:36:51,696
This decline is amplified in breastfed infants and may lead to newborn hemorrhage .

319
00:36:51,696 --> 00:37:02,946
Schott (2018) and Konstantinidi (2019) and their colleagues have provided thromboelastographic parameters for both healthy and sick term newborns.

320
00:37:02,946 --> 00:37:11,946
Fetal fibrinogen, which appears as early as 5 weeks, has the same amino acid composition as adult fibrinogen but different properties .

321
00:37:11,946 --> 00:37:18,109
It forms a less compressible clot, and the fibrin monomer has a lower degree of aggregation .

322
00:37:18,109 --> 00:37:26,909
Although plasma fibrinogen levels at birth are less than those in nonpregnant adults, the protein is functionally more active than adult fibrinogen .

323
00:37:26,909 --> 00:37:34,046
Neonates have higher cord plasma levels and fibronectin-fibrinogen complexes compared with maternal levels .

324
00:37:34,046 --> 00:37:42,446
Levels of functional fetal factor XIII (fibrin stabilizing factor) are significantly reduced compared with those in adults .

325
00:37:42,446 --> 00:37:52,784
Nielsen (1969) described low levels of plasminogen and elevated fibrinolytic activity in cord plasma compared with that of maternal plasma.

326
00:37:52,784 --> 00:37:57,834
Platelet counts in cord blood are in the normal range for nonpregnant adults.

327
00:37:57,834 --> 00:38:05,846
Despite this relative reduction in procoagulants, the fetus appears to be protected from hemorrhage, and fetal bleeding is rare.

328
00:38:05,846 --> 00:38:12,296
Even after invasive fetal procedures such as cordocentesis, excessive bleeding is uncommon.

329
00:38:12,296 --> 00:38:23,959
Ney and coworkers (1989) have shown that amnionic fluid thromboplastins and a factor(s) in Wharton jelly combine to aid coagulation at the umbilical cord puncture site.

330
00:38:23,959 --> 00:38:29,796
Various thrombophilias may cause thromboses and pregnancy complications in adults .

331
00:38:29,796 --> 00:38:37,684
If the fetus inherits one of these mutations, thrombosis and infarction can develop in the placenta or fetal organs.

332
00:38:37,684 --> 00:38:41,396
This is usually seen with homozygous inheritance.

333
00:38:41,396 --> 00:38:44,071
One example is homozygous

334
00:38:44,236 --> 00:38:48,673
protein C mutation, which causes purpura fulminans.

335
00:38:48,623 --> 00:38:57,523
Plasma Proteins Liver enzymes and other plasma proteins are produced by the fetus, and these levels do not correlate with maternal levels .

336
00:38:57,523 --> 00:39:09,173
Concentrations of plasma proteins, which include albumin, lactic dehydrogenase, aspartate and alanine aminotransferases, and γ-glutamyl transpeptidase, all rise.

337
00:39:09,173 --> 00:39:13,798
Conversely, prealbumin levels decline with gestational age .

338
00:39:13,798 --> 00:39:21,298
At birth, mean total plasma protein and albumin concentrations in fetal blood are similar to maternal levels.

339
00:39:21,298 --> 00:39:27,798
This is important because albumin binds unconjugated bilirubin to prevent kernicterus in the newborn .

340
00:39:27,798 --> 00:39:30,473
# Respiratory System.

341
00:39:30,473 --> 00:39:38,748
Lung maturation and biochemical indices of functional fetal lung maturity are important predictors of early neonatal outcome.

342
00:39:38,748 --> 00:39:45,336
Morphological or functional immaturity at birth leads to the development of the respiratory distress syndrome .

343
00:39:45,336 --> 00:39:54,661
A sufficient amount of surface-active materials —collectively referred to as surfactant—in the amnionic fluid is evidence of fetal lung maturity .

344
00:39:54,661 --> 00:40:05,711
As Liggins (1994) emphasized, however, the structural and morphological maturation of fetal lung also is extraordinarily important to proper lung function.

345
00:40:05,711 --> 00:40:12,823
Anatomical Maturation Like the branching of a tree, lung development proceeds along an established timetable.

346
00:40:12,823 --> 00:40:18,973
As with other organ systems, gene activation and deactivation control these functions .

347
00:40:18,973 --> 00:40:25,148
The lung primordium is an outgrowth from foregut endoderm at approximately 20 days’ gestation.

348
00:40:25,148 --> 00:40:34,198
The lung bud arises at 25 days and within this framework, four essential lung development stages are described by Moore (2020).

349
00:40:34,198 --> 00:40:41,961
First, the pseudoglandular stage entails growth of the intrasegmental bronchial tree between the 5th and 17th weeks.

350
00:40:41,961 --> 00:40:47,873
The microvasculature begins to develop, and the lung looks microscopically like a gland.

351
00:40:47,873 --> 00:40:56,148
Second, during the canalicular stage, from 16 to 25 weeks, the bronchial cartilage plates extend peripherally.

352
00:40:56,148 --> 00:41:04,686
Each terminal bronchiole gives rise to several respiratory bronchioles, and each of these in turn divides into multiple saccular ducts.

353
00:41:04,686 --> 00:41:09,323
Third, the terminal sac stage begins after 25 weeks.

354
00:41:09,323 --> 00:41:17,273
During this stage, primordial alveoli give rise to primitive pulmonary alveoli, that is, the terminal sacs.

355
00:41:17,273 --> 00:41:24,386
Simultaneously, an extracellular matrix develops from proximal to distal lung segments until term.

356
00:41:24,386 --> 00:41:30,673
The fourth alveolar stage begins during the late fetal period and continues well into childhood.

357
00:41:30,673 --> 00:41:38,911
An extensive capillary network is built, the lymphatic system forms, and type II pneumocytes begin to produce surfactant.

358
00:41:38,911 --> 00:41:44,886
At birth, only approximately 15 percent of the adult number of alveoli is present.

359
00:41:44,886 --> 00:41:50,173
Thus, the lung continues to grow and add more alveoli for up to 8 years.

360
00:41:50,173 --> 00:41:55,811
Various insults can upset this process, and their timing determines the sequelae.

361
00:41:55,811 --> 00:42:06,686
During the embryonic phase, abnormalities in lung development include esophageal and tracheal atresia, tracheoesophageal fistula, and pulmonary agenesis.

362
00:42:06,686 --> 00:42:17,161
Another example is fetal renal agenesis in which amnionic fluid is absent at the beginning of lung growth, and major defects occur in all four developmental stages.

363
00:42:17,161 --> 00:42:29,436
Similarly, the fetus with membrane rupture and subsequent oligohydramnios before 20 weeks usually exhibits nearly normal bronchial branching and cartilage development but has immature alveoli.

364
00:42:29,436 --> 00:42:36,886
In contrast, membrane rupture after 24 weeks may have minimal long-term effect on pulmonary structure.

365
00:42:36,886 --> 00:42:45,898
Last, vitamin D is thought to be important for several aspects of lung development (Hart, 2015; Ustun, 2020).

366
00:42:45,898 --> 00:42:57,198
Pulmonary Surfactant After the first breath, the terminal sacs must remain expanded despite the pressure imparted by the tissue-to-air interface, and surfactant keeps them from collapsing.

367
00:42:57,198 --> 00:43:01,886
Surfactant is formed in type II pneumocytes that line the alveoli.

368
00:43:01,886 --> 00:43:09,248
These cells are characterized by multivesicular bodies that produce the lamellar bodies in which surfactant is assembled.

369
00:43:09,248 --> 00:43:21,211
During late fetal life, at a time when the alveolus is characterized by a water-to-tissue interface, the intact lamellar bodies are secreted from the lung and swept into the amnionic fluid during

370
00:43:21,364 --> 00:43:25,089
respiratory-like movements that are termed fetal breathing.

371
00:43:25,039 --> 00:43:31,239
At birth, with the first breath, an air-to-tissue interface is established in the lung alveolus.

372
00:43:31,239 --> 00:43:39,114
Surfactant uncoils from the lamellar bodies and spreads to line the alveolus to prevent alveolar collapse during expiration.

373
00:43:39,114 --> 00:43:44,776
Thus, the fetal lungs’ capacity to produce surfactant establishes lung maturity.

374
00:43:44,776 --> 00:43:47,176
Surfactant Composition.

375
00:43:47,176 --> 00:43:59,064
Gluck (1972) and Hallman (1976) and their coworkers approximated that 90 percent of surfactant dry weight is lipid, specifically glycerophospholipids.

376
00:43:59,064 --> 00:44:02,239
Proteins account for the other 10 percent.

377
00:44:02,239 --> 00:44:08,051
Nearly 80 percent of the glycerophospholipids are phosphatidylcholines (lecithins).

378
00:44:08,051 --> 00:44:18,276
The principal active component that constitutes half of surfactant is a specific lecithin, which is dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine (DPPC or PC).

379
00:44:18,276 --> 00:44:23,614
Phosphatidylglycerol (PG) accounts for another 8 to 15 percent.

380
00:44:23,614 --> 00:44:28,964
Its precise role is unclear because newborns without PG usually do well.

381
00:44:28,964 --> 00:44:33,401
The other major constituent is phosphatidylinositol (PI).

382
00:44:33,401 --> 00:44:35,801
Surfactant Synthesis.

383
00:44:35,801 --> 00:44:39,814
Biosynthesis takes place in the type II pneumocytes.

384
00:44:39,814 --> 00:44:49,914
The apoproteins are produced in the endoplasmic reticulum, and the glycerophospholipids are synthesized by cooperative interactions of several cellular organelles.

385
00:44:49,914 --> 00:44:59,401
Phospholipid is the primary surface tension–lowering component of surfactant, whereas the apoproteins aid the forming and reforming of a surface film.

386
00:44:59,401 --> 00:45:08,601
The major apoprotein is surfactant A (SP-A), which is a glycoprotein with a molecular weight of 28,000 to 35,000 Da.

387
00:45:08,601 --> 00:45:17,364
It is synthesized in the type II cells, and its content in amnionic fluid increases with gestational age and fetal lung maturity.

388
00:45:17,364 --> 00:45:21,839
SP-A gene expression is demonstrable by 29 weeks .

389
00:45:21,839 --> 00:45:30,801
Specifically, SP-A1 and SP-A2 are two separate genes on chromosome 10, and their regulation is distinctive and different .

390
00:45:30,801 --> 00:45:34,389
Corticosteroids and Fetal Lung Maturation.

391
00:45:34,389 --> 00:45:49,539
Since Liggins (1969) observed accelerated lung maturation in lamb fetuses given glucocorticosteroid prior to preterm delivery, many suggested that fetal cortisol stimulates lung maturation and surfactant synthesis.

392
00:45:49,539 --> 00:45:56,739
It is unlikely, however, that corticosteroids are the only stimulus for augmented surfactant formation.

393
00:45:56,739 --> 00:46:03,701
But, when these are administered at certain critical times, they may improve preterm fetal lung maturation.

394
00:46:03,701 --> 00:46:14,289
Antenatal betamethasone and dexamethasone for lung maturation and neonatal replacement surfactant therapy are discussed in Chapter 34 (p. 617).

395
00:46:14,289 --> 00:46:22,664
Breathing Fetal respiratory muscles develop early, and chest wall movements are detected sonographically as early as 11 weeks .

396
00:46:22,664 --> 00:46:26,589
Breathing is essential for normal lung growth and development.

397
00:46:26,589 --> 00:46:36,301
From the beginning of the fourth month, the fetus engages in respiratory movement sufficiently intense to move amnionic fluid in and out of the respiratory tract.

398
00:46:36,301 --> 00:46:43,276
Some extrauterine events have effects on fetal breathing, for example, maternal exercise stimulates it .

399
00:46:43,276 --> 00:46:45,864
# Digestive System.

400
00:46:45,864 --> 00:46:55,014
After its embryogenic formation from the yolk sac as the primordial gut, the digestive system forms the intestines and various appendages.

401
00:46:55,014 --> 00:47:05,001
The foregut gives rise to the pharynx, lower respiratory system, esophagus, stomach, proximal duodenum, liver, pancreas, and biliary tree.

402
00:47:05,001 --> 00:47:12,201
The midgut gives rise to the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, and the right colon.

403
00:47:12,201 --> 00:47:20,014
The hindgut develops into the left colon, rectum, and the superior portion of the anal canal that empties into the cloaca .

404
00:47:20,014 --> 00:47:26,939
Numerous malformations develop in these structures from improper rotation, fixation, and partitioning.

405
00:47:26,939 --> 00:47:35,364
A common example is one of the several types of intestinal atresias (Moore, 2020; Stoll, 2017).

406
00:47:35,364 --> 00:47:44,889
Swallowing begins at 10 to 12 weeks, coincident with the ability of the small intestine to undergo peristalsis and to actively transport glucose .

407
00:47:44,889 --> 00:47:51,664
As a correlate, neonates born preterm may have swallowing difficulties because of immature gut motility .

408
00:47:51,664 --> 00:47:58,164
Much of the water in swallowed fluid is absorbed, and unabsorbed matter is propelled to the lower colon.

409
00:47:58,164 --> 00:48:08,064
Gitlin (1974) demonstrated that late in pregnancy, approximately 800 mg of soluble protein is ingested daily by the

410
00:48:08,212 --> 00:48:09,787
fetus.

411
00:48:09,737 --> 00:48:20,399
The stimulus for swallowing is unclear, but the fetal neural analogue of thirst, gastric emptying, and change in the amnionic fluid composition are potential factors .

412
00:48:20,399 --> 00:48:30,312
The fetal taste buds may play a role because saccharin injected into amnionic fluid increases swallowing, whereas injection of a noxious chemical inhibits it .

413
00:48:30,312 --> 00:48:39,262
Fetal swallowing appears to have little effect on amnionic fluid volume early in pregnancy because the volume swallowed is small compared with the total.

414
00:48:39,262 --> 00:48:48,149
However, term fetuses swallow between 200 and 760 mL per day —an amount comparable to that of the term neonate .

415
00:48:48,149 --> 00:48:54,824
Thus at term, amnionic fluid volume regulation can be substantially altered by fetal swallowing.

416
00:48:54,824 --> 00:49:03,174
For example, as discussed in Chapter 14 (p. 256), if swallowing is inhibited, hydramnios is common.

417
00:49:03,174 --> 00:49:11,174
Hydrochloric acid and some digestive enzymes are present in the stomach and small intestine in minimal amounts in the early fetus.

418
00:49:11,174 --> 00:49:17,137
Intrinsic factor is detectable by 11 weeks, and pepsinogen by 16 weeks.

419
00:49:17,137 --> 00:49:24,324
The preterm neonate, depending on its gestational age, may have transient deficiencies of these enzymes.

420
00:49:24,324 --> 00:49:28,224
The small intestinal histological appearance is normal .

421
00:49:28,224 --> 00:49:32,912
Stomach emptying appears to be stimulated primarily by volume.

422
00:49:32,912 --> 00:49:36,187
A dilated stomach suggests obstruction .

423
00:49:36,187 --> 00:49:43,674
Movement of amnionic fluid through the gastrointestinal system may enhance growth and development of the alimentary canal.

424
00:49:43,674 --> 00:49:48,087
That said, other regulatory factors likely are involved.

425
00:49:48,087 --> 00:49:58,349
For example, anencephalic fetuses, in which swallowing is limited, often have normal amnionic fluid volume and normal-appearing gastrointestinal tract.

426
00:49:58,349 --> 00:50:10,324
Meconium Fetal bowel contents consist of various products of secretion, such as glycerophospholipids from the lung, desquamated fetal cells, lanugo, scalp hair, and vernix.

427
00:50:10,324 --> 00:50:15,149
It also contains undigested debris from swallowed amnionic fluid.

428
00:50:15,149 --> 00:50:20,324
The dark greenish-black color forms from bile pigments, especially biliverdin.

429
00:50:20,324 --> 00:50:26,737
Meconium can pass from normal bowel peristalsis in the mature fetus or from vagal stimulation.

430
00:50:26,737 --> 00:50:34,637
It can also pass when hypoxia stimulates arginine vasopressin (AVP) release from the fetal pituitary gland.

431
00:50:34,637 --> 00:50:41,187
AVP stimulates colonic smooth muscle to contract, resulting in intraamnionic defecation .

432
00:50:41,187 --> 00:50:48,462
Meconium is toxic to the respiratory system, and its inhalation can result in meconium aspiration syndrome.

433
00:50:48,462 --> 00:50:54,049
Liver The hepatic diverticulum is an outgrowth of the endodermal lining of the foregut.

434
00:50:54,049 --> 00:50:59,912
Epithelial liver cords and primordial cells differentiate into hepatic parenchyma.

435
00:50:59,912 --> 00:51:04,499
By 9 weeks, the liver accounts for 10 percent of fetal weight .

436
00:51:04,499 --> 00:51:08,874
Serum liver enzyme levels increase with gestational age.

437
00:51:08,874 --> 00:51:17,587
As noted, in early gestation, fetal hepatic hemopoiesis is a key source of blood and immune cells (p. 125) .

438
00:51:17,587 --> 00:51:25,199
The fetal liver has a gestational-age-related capacity to conjugate bilirubin, which forms from hemoglobin breakdown .

439
00:51:25,199 --> 00:51:32,912
Because of hepatic immaturity, the preterm newborn is at particular risk for unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia .

440
00:51:32,912 --> 00:51:42,437
And because the life span of normal fetal macrocytic erythrocytes is shorter than that of the adult, relatively more unconjugated bilirubin is produced.

441
00:51:42,437 --> 00:51:52,724
Of this unconjugated form, only a small fraction is conjugated by the fetal liver, and this is excreted into the intestine and ultimately oxidized to biliverdin.

442
00:51:52,724 --> 00:52:01,562
Instead, most of the unconjugated bilirubin is excreted into the amnionic fluid after 12 weeks and transferred across the placenta .

443
00:52:01,562 --> 00:52:06,049
Importantly, placental bilirubin transfer is bidirectional.

444
00:52:06,049 --> 00:52:15,012
Thus, a woman with severe hemolysis has excess unconjugated bilirubin that readily passes to the fetus and then into the amnionic fluid.

445
00:52:15,012 --> 00:52:22,012
Conversely, conjugated bilirubin is not exchanged to any significant degree between mother and fetus.

446
00:52:22,012 --> 00:52:33,274
Most fetal cholesterol derives from hepatic synthesis, which satisfies the large demand for low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol by the fetal adrenal glands.

447
00:52:33,274 --> 00:52:48,424
However, an estimated 20 to 50 percent of fetal cholesterol originates from the mother, is transferred through the placenta, and released to circulating fetal apolipoproteins (Baardman, 2012; Pecks, 2014).

448
00:52:48,424 --> 00:52:50,587
Fetuses with growth

449
00:52:50,740 --> 00:52:57,815
restriction have lower cholesterol levels due to diminished fetal synthesis rather than diminished maternal supply .

450
00:52:57,765 --> 00:53:10,752
Hepatic glycogen is present in low concentration during the second trimester, but near term, levels rise rapidly and markedly to reach concentrations that are two- to threefold higher than those in the adult liver.

451
00:53:10,752 --> 00:53:14,865
After birth, glycogen content falls precipitously.

452
00:53:14,865 --> 00:53:21,540
Pancreas This gland arises from dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds from the endoderm of the foregut .

453
00:53:21,540 --> 00:53:25,227
Gene regulation of its development has been reviewed .

454
00:53:25,227 --> 00:53:33,840
Insulin-containing granules can be identified by 9 to 10 weeks, and insulin is detectable in fetal plasma at 12 weeks .

455
00:53:33,840 --> 00:53:44,852
Between 19 and 36 weeks, Kivilevitch and associates (2017) were able to visualize the pancreas sonographically in 60 percent of fetuses.

456
00:53:44,852 --> 00:53:49,802
The fetal pancreas responds to hyperglycemia by secreting insulin .

457
00:53:49,802 --> 00:53:55,640
Islets of Langerhans are enlarged in fetuses of mothers with metabolic abnormalities .

458
00:53:55,640 --> 00:54:01,765
These fetuses likely can be identified by sonographic pancreatic hyperechogenicity .

459
00:54:01,765 --> 00:54:06,340
Glucagon has been identified in the fetal pancreas at 8 weeks.

460
00:54:06,340 --> 00:54:15,002
Although hypoglycemia does not cause an increase in fetal glucagon levels, similar stimuli do so by 12 hours after birth .

461
00:54:15,002 --> 00:54:19,277
Most pancreatic enzymes are present by 16 weeks.

462
00:54:19,277 --> 00:54:29,090
Trypsin, chymotrypsin, phospholipase A, and lipase are found in the 14-week fetus, and their concentrations increase with gestational age .

463
00:54:29,090 --> 00:54:33,890
Amylase has been identified in amnionic fluid at 14 weeks .

464
00:54:33,890 --> 00:54:37,815
The exocrine function of the fetal pancreas is limited.

465
00:54:37,815 --> 00:54:47,790
Physiologically important secretion occurs only after stimulation by a secretagogue such as acetylcholine, which is released locally after vagal stimulation .

466
00:54:47,790 --> 00:54:55,115
Cholecystokinin normally is released only after protein ingestion and thus ordinarily is not found in the fetus.

467
00:54:55,115 --> 00:54:57,690
# Urinary System.

468
00:54:57,690 --> 00:55:06,940
Renal development involves interaction between pluripotential stem cells, undifferentiated mesenchymal cells, and epithelial components .

469
00:55:06,940 --> 00:55:15,877
Two primitive urinary systems—the pronephros and the mesonephros —precede development of the metanephros, which forms the final kidney.

470
00:55:15,877 --> 00:55:24,452
The pronephros involutes by 2 weeks, and the mesonephros produces urine at 5 weeks and degenerates by 11 to 12 weeks.

471
00:55:24,452 --> 00:55:31,552
Failure of these two structures either to form or to regress may result in anomalous urinary system development.

472
00:55:31,552 --> 00:55:39,090
Between 9 and 12 weeks, the ureteric bud and the nephrogenic blastema interact to produce the metanephros.

473
00:55:39,090 --> 00:55:43,177
Glomeruli develop and filtration begins by week 9 .

474
00:55:43,177 --> 00:55:47,152
The kidney and ureter develop from intermediate mesoderm.

475
00:55:47,152 --> 00:55:51,340
The bladder and urethra develop from the urogenital sinus.

476
00:55:51,340 --> 00:55:55,190
The bladder also develops in part from the allantois.

477
00:55:55,190 --> 00:55:59,252
Urogenital embryology is a focus of Chapter 3.

478
00:55:59,252 --> 00:56:04,577
By week 14, the loop of Henle is functional and reabsorption occurs .

479
00:56:04,577 --> 00:56:10,852
New nephrons continue to be formed until 36 weeks (Lindström, 2018).

480
00:56:10,852 --> 00:56:15,377
In preterm neonates, their formation continues after birth.

481
00:56:15,377 --> 00:56:23,327
Although the fetal kidneys produce urine, their ability to concentrate and modify the pH is limited even in the mature fetus.

482
00:56:23,327 --> 00:56:29,790
Fetal urine is hypotonic with respect to fetal plasma and has low electrolyte concentrations.

483
00:56:29,790 --> 00:56:36,390
Renal vascular resistance is high, and the filtration fraction is low compared with adult values .

484
00:56:36,390 --> 00:56:49,602
Fetal renal blood flow and thus urine production are controlled or influenced by the renin-angiotensin system, the sympathetic nervous system, prostaglandins, kallikrein, and atrial natriuretic peptide.

485
00:56:49,602 --> 00:57:00,877
The glomerular filtration rate increases with gestational age from less than 0.1 mL/min at 12 weeks to 0.3 mL/min at 20 weeks.

486
00:57:00,877 --> 00:57:06,040
In later gestation, the rate remains constant when corrected for fetal weight .

487
00:57:06,040 --> 00:57:13,352
Hemorrhage or hypoxia generally decreases renal blood flow, glomerular filtration rate, and urine output.

488
00:57:13,352 --> 00:57:17,677
Urine usually is found in the bladder even in small fetuses.

489
00:57:17,677 --> 00:57:21,627
The fetal kidneys start producing urine at 12 weeks.

490
00:57:21,627 --> 00:57:30,840
By 18 weeks, they are producing 7 to 14 mL/d, and at term, this increases to 650 mL/d .

491
00:57:30,840 --> 00:57:40,665
Maternally administered furosemide augments fetal urine formation, whereas uteroplacental insufficiency, fetal-growth restriction, and other fetal

492
00:57:40,828 --> 00:57:43,165
disorders can lower it.

493
00:57:43,115 --> 00:57:53,490
Obstruction of the urethra, bladder, ureters, or renal pelves can damage renal parenchyma and distort fetal anatomy (Müller Brochut, 2014).

494
00:57:53,490 --> 00:58:02,265
Pathological correlates and prenatal therapy of urinary tract obstruction are discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 376).

495
00:58:02,265 --> 00:58:09,628
Kidneys are not essential for survival in utero but influence control of amnionic fluid composition and volume.

496
00:58:09,628 --> 00:58:18,265
Thus, abnormalities that cause chronic fetal anuria are usually accompanied by oligohydramnios and pulmonary hypoplasia .

497
00:58:18,265 --> 00:58:21,415
# Endocrine Gland Development.

498
00:58:21,415 --> 00:58:27,940
The fetal endocrine system is functional for some time before the central nervous system reaches maturity .

499
00:58:27,940 --> 00:58:38,740
Pituitary Gland The anterior pituitary gland develops from oral ectoderm—the Rathke pouch —whereas the posterior pituitary gland derives from neuroectoderm.

500
00:58:38,740 --> 00:58:52,040
Embryonic development involves a complex and highly spatiotemporally regulated network of signaling molecules and transcription factors (Bancalari, 2012; Montenegro, 2019).

501
00:58:52,040 --> 00:58:55,003
Anterior and Intermediate Lobes.

502
00:58:55,003 --> 00:59:03,365
The adenohypophysis, or anterior pituitary, differentiates into five cell types that secrete six protein hormones.

503
00:59:03,365 --> 00:59:25,165
Of these types, lactotropes produce prolactin (PRL), somatotropes produce growth hormone (GH), corticotropes produce adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), thyrotropes produce thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), and gonadotropes produce luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH).

504
00:59:25,165 --> 00:59:34,178
ACTH is first detected in the fetal pituitary gland at 7 weeks, and GH and LH have been identified by 13 weeks.

505
00:59:34,178 --> 00:59:41,678
By the end of the 17th week, the fetal pituitary gland synthesizes and stores all pituitary hormones.

506
00:59:41,678 --> 00:59:49,428
Moreover, the fetal pituitary is responsive to tropic hormones and is capable of secreting these early in gestation .

507
00:59:49,428 --> 01:00:00,865
The fetal pituitary secretes βendorphin, and cord blood levels of β-endorphin and β-lipotropin rise with fetal arterial partial pressure of carbon dioxide (Paco2) .

508
01:00:00,865 --> 01:00:05,590
The intermediate lobe in the fetal pituitary gland is well developed.

509
01:00:05,590 --> 01:00:11,678
The cells of this structure begin to disappear before term and are absent from the adult pituitary.

510
01:00:11,678 --> 01:00:13,903
Neurohypophysis.

511
01:00:13,903 --> 01:00:24,003
The posterior pituitary gland or neurohypophysis is well developed by 10 to 12 weeks, and oxytocin and arginine vasopressin are demonstrable.

512
01:00:24,003 --> 01:00:32,165
Both hormones probably function in the fetus to conserve water by actions directed largely at the lung and placenta rather than kidney.

513
01:00:32,165 --> 01:00:38,153
Vasopressin levels in umbilical cord plasma are strikingly higher than maternal levels .

514
01:00:38,153 --> 01:00:44,265
Thyroid Gland The thyroid primordium arises from the endoderm of the primordial pharynx .

515
01:00:44,265 --> 01:00:52,253
The thyroid migrates to its final position, and the obliterated thyroglossal duct connects to the foramen cecum of the tongue.

516
01:00:52,253 --> 01:00:57,503
The pituitary–thyroid system is functional by the end of the first trimester.

517
01:00:57,503 --> 01:01:11,228
By 10 to 12 weeks, the thyroid gland is able to synthesize hormones, and thyrotropin, thyroxine, and thyroid-binding globulin (TBG) have been detected in fetal serum as early as 11 weeks .

518
01:01:11,228 --> 01:01:15,028
Thyroid follicles have formed and colloid is present.

519
01:01:15,028 --> 01:01:26,765
The placenta actively concentrates iodine on the fetal side, and by 12 weeks and throughout pregnancy, the fetal thyroid concentrates iodine more avidly than does the maternal thyroid.

520
01:01:26,765 --> 01:01:35,165
Thus, maternal administration of either radioiodine or appreciable amounts of ordinary iodine is hazardous after this time .

521
01:01:35,165 --> 01:01:44,865
Normal fetal levels of free thyroxine (T4), free triiodothyronine (T3), and TBG rise steadily throughout gestation .

522
01:01:44,865 --> 01:01:56,440
Compared with adult levels, by 36 weeks, fetal serum concentrations of TSH are higher, total and free T3 concentrations are lower, and T4 is similar.

523
01:01:56,440 --> 01:02:02,803
This suggests that the fetal pituitary may not become sensitive to feedback until late pregnancy.

524
01:02:02,803 --> 01:02:14,153
Fetal thyroid hormone plays a role in the normal development of virtually all fetal tissues, especially the brain (Andersen, 2018; Jansen, 2019).

525
01:02:14,153 --> 01:02:21,028
Congenital hypothyroidism is a heterogenous disorder for which several candidate genes have been identified .

526
01:02:21,028 --> 01:02:29,878
With hypothyroidism, it was previously believed that normal fetal growth and development provided evidence that T4 was not essential for

527
01:02:30,028 --> 01:02:31,853
fetal growth.

528
01:02:31,803 --> 01:02:46,428
It is now known, however, that growth proceeds normally because small quantities of maternal T4 prevent antenatal cretinism in fetuses with thyroid agenesis (Forhead, 2014; Vulsma, 1989).

529
01:02:46,428 --> 01:02:57,315
As discussed in Chapter 61 (p. 1098), the fetus with congenital hypothyroidism typically does not develop stigmata of cretinism until after birth .

530
01:02:57,315 --> 01:03:06,053
Because administration of thyroid hormone will prevent this, by state law, all newborns are tested for high serum levels of TSH .

531
01:03:06,053 --> 01:03:18,803
The placenta prevents substantial passage of maternal thyroid hormones to the fetus by rapidly deiodinating maternal T4 and T3 to form reverse T3, a relatively inactive thyroid hormone.

532
01:03:18,803 --> 01:03:24,690
Several antithyroid antibodies cross the placenta when present in high concentrations .

533
01:03:24,690 --> 01:03:35,615
Those include the long-acting thyroid stimulators (LATS), LATS-protector (LATS-P), and thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin (TSI).

534
01:03:35,615 --> 01:03:45,190
Congenital hyperthyroidism develops when maternal thyroid-stimulating antibody crosses the placenta to stimulate the fetal gland to secrete thyroxine .

535
01:03:45,190 --> 01:03:52,028
These fetuses develop large goiters as shown in Figure 61-3 (p. 1092).

536
01:03:52,028 --> 01:04:01,065
They also display tachycardia, hepatosplenomegaly, hematological abnormalities, craniosynostosis, and growth restriction.

537
01:04:01,065 --> 01:04:07,328
As children, they have perceptual motor difficulties, hyperactivity, and reduced growth .

538
01:04:07,328 --> 01:04:13,103
Immediately after birth, thyroid function and metabolism undergo major change.

539
01:04:13,103 --> 01:04:18,740
Cooling to room temperature evokes a sudden and marked increase in TSH secretion.

540
01:04:18,740 --> 01:04:26,815
This in turn causes a progressive increase in serum T4 levels that are maximal 24 to 36 hours after birth.

541
01:04:26,815 --> 01:04:31,565
There are nearly simultaneous elevations of serum T3 levels.

542
01:04:31,565 --> 01:04:35,965
Adrenal Glands These glands develop from two separate tissues.

543
01:04:35,965 --> 01:04:43,490
The medulla derives from neural crest ectoderm, whereas the fetal and adult cortex arise from intermediate mesoderm.

544
01:04:43,490 --> 01:04:52,128
The gland grows rapidly through cell proliferation and angiogenesis, cellular migration, hypertrophy, and apoptosis .

545
01:04:52,128 --> 01:05:00,553
Expression of the Kiss1R gene, alone or in concert with corticotropinreleasing hormone, stimulates fetal adrenal growth .

546
01:05:00,553 --> 01:05:10,903
Fetal glands are enormous in relation to body size and are 10 to 20 times larger than adult glands (Karsli, 2019; Moore, 2020).

547
01:05:10,903 --> 01:05:17,615
The bulk is made up of the inner or fetal zone of the adrenal cortex and involutes rapidly after birth.

548
01:05:17,615 --> 01:05:24,640
This zone is scant to absent in rare instances in which the fetal pituitary gland is congenitally absent.

549
01:05:24,640 --> 01:05:30,528
The function of the fetal adrenal glands is discussed in Chapter 5 (p. 102).

550
01:05:30,528 --> 01:05:33,340
# Immunological System.

551
01:05:33,340 --> 01:05:40,103
Infections in utero have provided an opportunity to examine mechanisms of the fetal immune response .

552
01:05:40,103 --> 01:05:45,553
Evidence of immunological competence has been reported as early as 13 weeks .

553
01:05:45,553 --> 01:05:52,853
In cord blood at or near term, the average level for most components approximates half that of the adult values.

554
01:05:52,853 --> 01:06:02,753
B cells differentiate from pluripotent hemopoietic stem cells that migrate to the liver (Berthault, 2017; Melchers, 2015).

555
01:06:02,753 --> 01:06:15,403
Despite this, in the absence of a direct antigenic stimulus such as infection, fetal plasma immunoglobulins consist almost totally of transferred maternal immunoglobulin G (IgG).

556
01:06:15,403 --> 01:06:21,653
Thus, antibodies in the newborn most often reflect maternal immunological experiences .

557
01:06:21,653 --> 01:06:28,503
The interaction between maternal and fetal T cells is described in Chapter 5 (p. 93).

558
01:06:28,503 --> 01:06:35,303
Immunoglobulin G Maternal IgG transport correlates with placental Fc receptor expression .

559
01:06:35,303 --> 01:06:41,065
Fetal transport begins at approximately 16 weeks and increases thereafter.

560
01:06:41,065 --> 01:06:50,003
Because the bulk of IgG is acquired during the last 4 weeks, preterm neonates are poorly endowed with protective maternal antibodies.

561
01:06:50,003 --> 01:06:56,840
Newborns begin to slowly produce IgG, and adult values are not attained until age 3 years.

562
01:06:56,840 --> 01:07:04,553
In certain situations, the transfer of IgG antibodies from mother to fetus can be harmful rather than protective.

563
01:07:04,553 --> 01:07:11,315
The classic example is hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn resulting from Rhantigen alloimmunization .

564
01:07:11,315 --> 01:07:23,778
Immunoglobulins M and A In the adult, production of immunoglobulin M (IgM) in response to an antigenic stimulus is superseded in a week or so predominantly by IgG production.

565
01:07:23,778 --> 01:07:27,153
Similarly, very little IgM is

566
01:07:27,316 --> 01:07:37,141
produced by normal fetuses not exposed to infection, but with infection, the IgM response predominates and remains so for weeks to months in the newborn.

567
01:07:37,091 --> 01:07:44,853
And, because IgM is not transported from the mother, any IgM in the fetus or newborn is that which it produced.

568
01:07:44,853 --> 01:07:52,091
Thus, specific IgM levels in umbilical cord blood may be elevated in those with congenital infection.

569
01:07:52,091 --> 01:08:07,353
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019), elevated IgM levels are usually found in newborns with congenital infection such as rubella, cytomegalovirus infection, or toxoplasmosis.

570
01:08:07,353 --> 01:08:12,991
In infants, adult levels of IgM are normally attained by age 9 months.

571
01:08:12,991 --> 01:08:20,841
Immunoglobulin A (IgA) ingested in colostrum provides mucosal protection against enteric infections.

572
01:08:20,841 --> 01:08:26,366
There is only a small amount of fetal secretory IgA found in amnionic fluid .

573
01:08:26,366 --> 01:08:38,691
Lymphocytes and Monocytes The immune system develops early, and B lymphocytes are derived from primordial stem cells and appear in fetal liver by 9 weeks and in blood and spleen by 12 weeks .

574
01:08:38,691 --> 01:08:43,716
T lymphocytes begin to leave the thymus at approximately 14 weeks.

575
01:08:43,716 --> 01:08:51,853
Despite this, the newborn responds poorly to immunization, and especially poorly to bacterial capsular polysaccharides.

576
01:08:51,853 --> 01:09:02,791
This immature response may stem from a deficient response of newborn B cells to polyclonal activators or from a lack of T cells that proliferate in response to specific stimuli .

577
01:09:02,791 --> 01:09:10,328
In the newborn, monocytes are able to process and present antigen when tested with maternal antigenspecific T cells.

578
01:09:10,328 --> 01:09:20,803
DNA methylation patterns are developmentally regulated during monocyte-macrophage differentiation and contribute to the antiinflammatory phenotype in macrophages .

579
01:09:20,803 --> 01:09:23,828
# Musculoskeletal System.

580
01:09:23,828 --> 01:09:27,591
The origin of most muscles and bones is mesodermal.

581
01:09:27,591 --> 01:09:35,628
MYOD is a member of the family of myogenic regulatory factors that activates transcription of muscle-specific genes .

582
01:09:35,628 --> 01:09:38,641
The limb buds appear by the fourth week.

583
01:09:38,641 --> 01:09:43,903
Most skeletal muscle derives from myogenic precursor cells in the somites.

584
01:09:43,903 --> 01:09:52,541
The skeleton arises from condensed mesenchyme—embryonic connective tissue—which eventually forms hyaline cartilage models of the bones.

585
01:09:52,541 --> 01:09:56,778
Osteoclasts arise from erythro-myeloid progenitors .

586
01:09:56,778 --> 01:10:04,666
By the end of the embryonic period, ossification centers have developed, and bones harden by endochondral ossification.

587
01:10:04,666 --> 01:10:06,766
Energy And Nutrition.

588
01:10:06,766 --> 01:10:15,141
Because of the small amount of yolk in the human ovum, growth of the embryofetus is dependent on maternal nutrients during the first 2 months.

589
01:10:15,141 --> 01:10:24,528
During the first few days after implantation, blastocyst nutrition comes from the interstitial fluid of the endometrium and the surrounding maternal tissue.

590
01:10:24,528 --> 01:10:31,741
Maternal adaptations to store and transfer nutrients to the fetus are discussed in Chapter 4 and summarized here.

591
01:10:31,741 --> 01:10:37,316
Three major maternal storage depots are the liver, muscle, and adipose tissue.

592
01:10:37,316 --> 01:10:45,128
These maternal depots and the storage hormone insulin are intimately involved in the metabolism of the nutrients absorbed from the gut.

593
01:10:45,128 --> 01:10:51,678
Maternal insulin secretion is sustained by increased serum levels of glucose and amino acids.

594
01:10:51,678 --> 01:11:02,328
The net effect is maternal storage of glucose as glycogen primarily in liver and muscle, retention of some amino acids as protein, and storage of the excess as fat .

595
01:11:02,328 --> 01:11:10,216
Storage of maternal fat peaks in the second trimester and then declines as fetal energy demands rise in the third trimester .

596
01:11:10,216 --> 01:11:24,053
Interestingly, the placenta appears to act as a nutrient sensor, altering transport based on the maternal supply and environmental stimuli (Jansson, 2006b, Wesolowski, 2017).

597
01:11:24,053 --> 01:11:35,966
During times of fasting, glucose is released from glycogen, but maternal glycogen stores cannot provide an adequate amount of glucose to meet requirements for maternal energy and fetal growth.

598
01:11:35,966 --> 01:11:46,041
Augmentation is provided by cleavage of triacylglycerols, stored in adipose tissue, which results in free fatty acids and activation of lipolysis.

599
01:11:46,041 --> 01:11:49,141
# Glucose and Fetal Growth.

600
01:11:49,141 --> 01:11:56,378
Although dependent on the mother for nutrition, the fetus also actively participates in providing its own nutrition.

601
01:11:56,378 --> 01:12:03,528
At midpregnancy, fetal serum glucose concentration is independent of maternal levels and may exceed

602
01:12:03,700 --> 01:12:05,062
them.

603
01:12:05,012 --> 01:12:09,175
Glucose is the major nutrient for fetal growth and energy.

604
01:12:09,175 --> 01:12:17,187
Logically, mechanisms exist to minimize maternal glucose use so that the limited maternal supply is available to the fetus.

605
01:12:17,187 --> 01:12:27,287
As one example, human placental lactogen (hPL) is a hormone normally abundant in the mother but not the fetus and has an insulin antagonist effect.

606
01:12:27,287 --> 01:12:36,137
It blocks the peripheral uptake and use of glucose, while instead promoting mobilization and use of free fatty acids by maternal tissues .

607
01:12:36,137 --> 01:12:42,450
This hormone is also diabetogenic as discussed in Chapter 60 (p. 1068).

608
01:12:42,450 --> 01:12:46,475
Last, intrinsic epigenetic changes play a role .

609
01:12:46,475 --> 01:12:53,875
For example, nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is associated with fetal macrosomia .

610
01:12:53,875 --> 01:13:05,200
Glucose Transport The transfer of D-glucose across cell membranes is accomplished by a carrier-mediated, stereospecific, nonconcentrating process of facilitated diffusion.

611
01:13:05,200 --> 01:13:15,212
There are 14 glucose transport proteins (GLUTs) encoded by the SLC2A gene family and characterized by tissue-specific distribution .

612
01:13:15,212 --> 01:13:18,562
Several of these are expressed by trophoblast .

613
01:13:18,562 --> 01:13:33,512
GLUT-1, GLUT-3, and GLUT-4 primarily aid glucose uptake by the placenta and are located in the plasma membrane of the syncytiotrophoblast microvilli (Acosta, 2015; James-Allan, 2019).

614
01:13:33,512 --> 01:13:41,287
DNA methylation regulates expression of placental GLUT genes, with epigenetic modification across gestation .

615
01:13:41,287 --> 01:13:47,512
Methylation increases as pregnancy advances and is induced by almost all growth factors.

616
01:13:47,512 --> 01:13:53,650
In addition to its transport role, the placenta uses glucose for its metabolic functions.

617
01:13:53,650 --> 01:13:58,000
Fetal and placental glucose consumption are inversely related.

618
01:13:58,000 --> 01:14:03,537
Thus, placental glucose use is a key modulator of maternal-fetal transfer .

619
01:14:03,537 --> 01:14:10,775
Lactate is a product of glucose metabolism and transported across the placenta also by facilitated diffusion.

620
01:14:10,775 --> 01:14:17,325
By way of cotransport with hydrogen ions, lactate is probably transported as lactic acid.

621
01:14:17,325 --> 01:14:24,975
Fetal Macrosomia The precise biomolecular events in the pathophysiology of fetal macrosomia are not defined.

622
01:14:24,975 --> 01:14:30,137
Nonetheless, fetal hyperinsulinemia is clearly one driving force .

623
01:14:30,137 --> 01:14:42,700
As discussed with fetal-growth disorders in Chapter 47 (p. 824), insulin-like growth factor and leptin and other adipokines are important regulators of placental development and function .

624
01:14:42,700 --> 01:14:46,250
Maternal obesity begets fetal macrosomia.

625
01:14:46,250 --> 01:14:56,725
In addition, theories suggest that maternal obesity affects fetal cardiomyocyte growth that may result in fetal cardiomyopathy or even congenital heart disease .

626
01:14:56,725 --> 01:14:58,800
# Leptin.

627
01:14:58,800 --> 01:15:08,037
This polypeptide hormone was originally identified as a product of adipocytes and a regulator of energy homeostasis by curbing appetite.

628
01:15:08,037 --> 01:15:18,700
It also contributes to angiogenesis, hemopoiesis, osteogenesis, pulmonary maturation, and neuroendocrine, immune, and reproductive functions .

629
01:15:18,700 --> 01:15:22,987
Leptin is produced by the mother, fetus, and placenta.

630
01:15:22,987 --> 01:15:28,612
It is expressed in syncytiotrophoblast and fetal vascular endothelial cells.

631
01:15:28,612 --> 01:15:36,525
Of placental production, 5 percent enters the fetal circulation, whereas 95 percent is transferred to the mother .

632
01:15:36,525 --> 01:15:41,337
Leptin concentrations peak in amnionic fluid at midpregnancy .

633
01:15:41,337 --> 01:15:48,337
Fetal serum leptin levels begin increasing at approximately 34 weeks and are correlated with fetal weight.

634
01:15:48,337 --> 01:16:02,312
This hormone is involved in the development and maturation of the heart, brain, kidneys, and pancreas, and its levels are decreased with fetal-growth restriction (Briffa, 2015; Yalinbas, 2019).

635
01:16:02,312 --> 01:16:15,137
Abnormal levels are associated with fetal-growth disorders, gestational diabetes, and preeclampsia, but not maternal obesity (Allbrand, 2018; Gurugubelli, 2018).

636
01:16:15,137 --> 01:16:19,637
Postpartum, leptin levels decline in both the newborn and mother.

637
01:16:19,637 --> 01:16:25,325
Perinatal leptin is associated with the development of metabolic syndromes later in life .

638
01:16:25,325 --> 01:16:29,075
# Free Fatty Acids and Triglycerides.

639
01:16:29,075 --> 01:16:35,237
The term newborn has a large proportion of fat, which averages 15 percent of body weight .

640
01:16:35,237 --> 01:16:42,350
Thus, late in pregnancy, a substantial part of the substrate transferred to the human fetus is stored as fat.

641
01:16:42,350 --> 01:16:53,087
Although maternal obesity raises placental fatty acid uptake and fetal fat deposition, it does not appear to affect fetal organ growth (Dubé, 2012).

642
01:16:53,087 --> 01:16:57,375
Neutral fat in the form of triglycerides does not cross the

643
01:16:57,532 --> 01:17:00,432
placenta, but glycerol does.

644
01:17:00,382 --> 01:17:10,869
Despite this, evidence supports that abnormal maternal concentrations of triglycerides— both low and high levels—are associated with major congenital anomalies .

645
01:17:10,869 --> 01:17:17,244
There is preferential placental-fetal transfer of long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids .

646
01:17:17,244 --> 01:17:23,007
Lipoprotein lipase is present on the maternal but not on the fetal side of the placenta.

647
01:17:23,007 --> 01:17:31,794
This arrangement favors hydrolysis of triacylglycerols in the maternal intervillous space yet preserves these neutral lipids in fetal blood.

648
01:17:31,794 --> 01:17:37,494
Fatty acids transferred to the fetus can be converted to triglycerides in the fetal liver.

649
01:17:37,494 --> 01:17:46,069
The placental uptake and use of LDL is an alternative mechanism for fetal assimilation of essential fatty acids and amino acids .

650
01:17:46,069 --> 01:17:52,894
LDL binds to specific receptors in the coated-pit regions of the syncytiotrophoblast microvilli.

651
01:17:52,894 --> 01:18:02,132
The large LDL particle, measuring about 250,000 Da, is taken up by a process of receptor-mediated endocytosis.

652
01:18:02,132 --> 01:18:21,682
The apoprotein and cholesterol esters of LDL are hydrolyzed by lysosomal enzymes in the syncytium to yield: (1) cholesterol for progesterone synthesis; (2) free amino acids, including essential amino acids; and (3) essential fatty acids, primarily linoleic acid.

653
01:18:21,682 --> 01:18:33,582
Interestingly, maternal cholesterol is obligatory for steroid hormone synthesis, but increasing levels are associated with fetal aortic atherogenesis (de Nigris, 2018).

654
01:18:33,582 --> 01:18:36,082
# Amino Acids.

655
01:18:36,082 --> 01:18:44,319
The placenta concentrates many amino acids in the syncytiotrophoblast, which are then transferred to the fetal side by diffusion.

656
01:18:44,319 --> 01:18:54,194
In cordocentesis blood samples, the amino acid concentration in umbilical cord plasma is greater than in maternal venous or arterial plasma.

657
01:18:54,194 --> 01:18:58,957
DNA methylation serves to regulate transporter gene expression .

658
01:18:58,957 --> 01:19:05,007
Transport system activity is influenced by gestational age and environmental factors.

659
01:19:05,007 --> 01:19:13,532
These include heat stress, hypoxia, under- and overnutrition, and hormones such as glucocorticoids, growth hormone, and leptin .

660
01:19:13,532 --> 01:19:25,044
Trophoblastic mammalian target of rapamycin complex 1 (mTORC1) regulates placental amino acid transporters and modulates transfer across the placenta .

661
01:19:25,044 --> 01:19:37,069
In vivo studies suggest an upregulation of transport for certain amino acids and a greater delivery rate of these to the fetuses of women with gestational diabetes associated with fetal overgrowth .

662
01:19:37,069 --> 01:19:39,294
# Proteins.

663
01:19:39,294 --> 01:19:44,432
Placental transfer of larger proteins is limited, but there are exceptions.

664
01:19:44,432 --> 01:19:52,682
As discussed, IgG crosses the placenta in large amounts via endocytosis and trophoblast Fc receptors.

665
01:19:52,682 --> 01:20:03,569
IgG transfer depends on maternal levels of total IgG, gestational age, placental integrity, IgG subclass, and antigenic potential .

666
01:20:03,569 --> 01:20:12,107
Conversely, the larger immunoglobulins—IgA and IgM— of maternal origin are effectively excluded from the fetus.

667
01:20:12,107 --> 01:20:14,982
# Ions and Trace Metals.

668
01:20:14,982 --> 01:20:19,719
Calcium and phosphorus are actively transported from mother to fetus.

669
01:20:19,719 --> 01:20:23,907
Calcium is transferred for fetal skeletal mineralization .

670
01:20:23,907 --> 01:20:27,707
A calcium-binding protein is produced in placenta.

671
01:20:27,707 --> 01:20:36,644
Parathyroid hormone– related protein (PTH-rP), as the name implies, acts as a surrogate PTH in many systems .

672
01:20:36,644 --> 01:20:45,569
PTH is not found in fetal plasma, but PTH-rP is present, suggesting that PTH-rP is the fetal parathormone .

673
01:20:45,569 --> 01:20:53,782
The expression of PTH-rP in cytotrophoblasts is modulated by the extracellular concentration of Ca2+ .

674
01:20:53,782 --> 01:21:04,932
It seems possible that PTH-rP synthesized in decidua, placenta, and other fetal tissues is important in fetal Ca2+ transfer and homeostasis.

675
01:21:04,932 --> 01:21:08,994
Another example is the unidirectional transfer of iron.

676
01:21:08,994 --> 01:21:14,694
Typically, maternal plasma iron concentration is much lower than that in her fetus.

677
01:21:14,694 --> 01:21:20,782
Even with severe maternal iron-deficiency anemia, the fetal hemoglobin mass is normal.

678
01:21:20,782 --> 01:21:27,607
Iodine transport is clearly attributable to a carrier-mediated, energyrequiring active process.

679
01:21:27,607 --> 01:21:34,719
And, as discussed in Chapter 61 (p. 1097), the placenta concentrates iodine .

680
01:21:34,719 --> 01:21:40,669
The concentrations of zinc in the fetal plasma also are greater than those in maternal plasma.

681
01:21:40,669 --> 01:21:46,432
Conversely, copper levels in fetal plasma are less than those in maternal plasma.

682
01:21:46,432 --> 01:21:53,744
This fact is of particular interest because important copper-requiring enzymes are necessary for fetal development.

683
01:21:53,908 --> 01:22:02,633
Placental Sequestration of Heavy Metals The heavy metal–binding protein metallothionein-1 is expressed in human syncytiotrophoblast.

684
01:22:02,583 --> 01:22:09,983
This protein binds and sequesters a host of heavy metals, including zinc, copper, lead, and cadmium.

685
01:22:09,983 --> 01:22:13,458
Despite this, fetal exposure is variable .

686
01:22:13,458 --> 01:22:20,370
For example, lead enters the fetal environment at a level 90 percent of maternal concentrations.

687
01:22:20,370 --> 01:22:24,770
In contrast, placental transfer of cadmium is limited .

688
01:22:24,770 --> 01:22:29,358
The most common source of environmental cadmium is cigarette smoke.

689
01:22:29,358 --> 01:22:34,445
Metallothionein also binds and sequesters copper in placental tissue.

690
01:22:34,445 --> 01:22:38,108
This accounts for the low copper levels in cord blood .

691
01:22:38,108 --> 01:22:43,420
It is possible that cadmium provokes metallothionein synthesis in the amnion.

692
01:22:43,420 --> 01:22:51,033
This may cause copper sequestration, a pseudocopper deficiency, and in turn, diminished amnion tensile strength.

693
01:22:51,033 --> 01:22:53,245
# Vitamins.

694
01:22:53,245 --> 01:23:02,445
The concentration of vitamin A (retinol) is greater in fetal than in maternal plasma and is bound to retinol-binding protein and to prealbumin.

695
01:23:02,445 --> 01:23:08,870
Retinolbinding protein is transferred from the maternal compartment across the syncytiotrophoblast.

696
01:23:08,870 --> 01:23:17,770
The transport of vitamin C—ascorbic acid—from mother to fetus is accomplished by an energy-dependent, carrier-mediated process.

697
01:23:17,770 --> 01:23:25,558
As a result, the concentration of ascorbic acid is two to four times higher in fetal plasma than in maternal plasma .

698
01:23:25,558 --> 01:23:35,720
Levels of principal vitamin D metabolites, including 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, are greater in maternal plasma than in fetal plasma.

699
01:23:35,720 --> 01:23:43,445
The 1β-hydroxylation of 25-hydroxyvitamin D3 is known to take place in placenta and in decidua.

700
01:23:43,445 --> 01:23:46,133
Placental Role In Embryofetal.

701
01:23:46,133 --> 01:23:47,795
Development.

702
01:23:47,795 --> 01:23:52,145
The placenta is the organ of transfer between mother and fetus.

703
01:23:52,145 --> 01:24:03,233
Within this bidirectional maternal-fetal interface, maternal oxygen and nutrients transfer to the fetus, whereas CO2 and metabolic wastes are directed back to the mother.

704
01:24:03,233 --> 01:24:13,320
Fetal blood, which is contained in the fetal capillaries of the chorionic villi, has no direct contact with maternal blood, which remains in the intervillous space.

705
01:24:13,320 --> 01:24:22,683
Instead, bidirectional transfer depends on processes that allow or aid the transport through the syncytiotrophoblast that lines chorionic villi .

706
01:24:22,683 --> 01:24:32,170
With this system, breaks in the chorionic villi permit escape of fetal/placental cells and other blood-borne material into the maternal circulation.

707
01:24:32,170 --> 01:24:39,870
This leakage is the mechanism by which some D-negative women become sensitized by the erythrocytes of their D-positive fetus .

708
01:24:39,870 --> 01:24:50,558
The escape of fetal cells can also lead to fetal microchimerism from entrance of allogeneic fetal cells, including trophoblast, into maternal blood and other organs .

709
01:24:50,558 --> 01:24:56,283
Volumes are estimated to range from 1 to 6 cells/mL at midpregnancy.

710
01:24:56,283 --> 01:25:03,145
Some fetal cells become “immortal” in that they persist in the maternal circulation and organs following pregnancy.

711
01:25:03,145 --> 01:25:15,495
As discussed in Chapter 61 (p. 1109), the clinical corollary is that some maternal autoimmune diseases such as Hashimoto thyroiditis may be provoked by such microchimerism.

712
01:25:15,495 --> 01:25:24,158
Last, cell-free DNA (cfDNA) is released from syncytiotrophoblast during normal physiological cell turnover.

713
01:25:24,158 --> 01:25:35,345
After 10 weeks, 10 to 15 percent of all cfDNA in maternal plasma is trophoblastic DNA (Norton, 2015; Shaffer, 2018).

714
01:25:35,345 --> 01:25:47,620
This phenomenon underlies one maternal-serum screening method for fetal aneuploidy, which is discussed in Chapters 16 and 17 (pp. 327 and 335).

715
01:25:47,620 --> 01:25:50,333
# The Intervillous Space.

716
01:25:50,333 --> 01:25:56,395
Maternal blood within the intervillous space is the primary source of maternal–fetal transfer.

717
01:25:56,395 --> 01:26:02,720
Blood from the maternal spiral arteries directly bathes the trophoblast layer that surrounds the villi.

718
01:26:02,720 --> 01:26:10,645
Substances transferred from mother to fetus first enter the intervillous space and are then transported to the syncytiotrophoblast.

719
01:26:10,645 --> 01:26:19,145
As such, the chorionic villi and intervillous space function together as the fetal lung, gastrointestinal tract, and kidney.

720
01:26:19,145 --> 01:26:25,370
Circulation within the intervillous space is described in Chapter 5 (p. 92).

721
01:26:25,370 --> 01:26:36,320
Intervillous and uteroplacental blood flow increases throughout pregnancy, and at term, the residual volume of the intervillous space approximates 140 mL.

722
01:26:36,320 --> 01:26:41,033
Moreover, uteroplacental blood flow near term ranges from

723
01:26:41,188 --> 01:26:48,275
700 to 900 mL/min, and most of this blood apparently goes to the intervillous space .

724
01:26:48,225 --> 01:26:50,888
# Placental Transfer.

725
01:26:50,888 --> 01:27:04,938
In the terminal villi, substances that pass from maternal to fetal blood must first traverse the syncytiotrophoblast, the attenuated cytotrophoblast layer, the thinned villous stroma, and finally, the fetal capillary wall.

726
01:27:04,938 --> 01:27:12,238
Although this histological barrier separates maternal and fetal circulations, it is not a simple physical barrier.

727
01:27:12,238 --> 01:27:22,725
First, throughout pregnancy, syncytiotrophoblast actively or passively permits, facilitates, and adjusts the amount and rate of substance transfer to the fetus.

728
01:27:22,725 --> 01:27:32,875
As discussed in Chapter 5 (p. 92), the maternal-facing syncytiotrophoblast surface is characterized by a complex microvillus structure.

729
01:27:32,875 --> 01:27:38,288
The fetal-facing basal cell membrane is the site of transfer to the intravillous space.

730
01:27:38,288 --> 01:27:46,538
Finally, the villous capillaries are an additional site for transport from the intravillous space into fetal blood, or vice versa.

731
01:27:46,538 --> 01:27:55,450
In determining the effectiveness of the human placenta as an organ of transfer, several variables are important and shown in Table 7-2.

732
01:27:55,450 --> 01:28:02,813
Epigenetic placental changes brought about by methylation of genes engaged in nutrient transfer also play a role .

733
01:28:02,813 --> 01:28:05,125
Table 7-2.

734
01:28:05,125 --> 01:28:16,688
Variables of Maternal-Fetal Substance Transfer Mechanisms of Transfer Most substances with a molecular mass <500 Da pass readily through placental tissue by simple diffusion.

735
01:28:16,688 --> 01:28:23,375
These include oxygen, CO2, water, most electrolytes, and anesthetic gases .

736
01:28:23,375 --> 01:28:29,625
Some low-molecularweight compounds undergo transfer facilitated by syncytiotrophoblast.

737
01:28:29,625 --> 01:28:36,775
These are usually those that have low concentrations in maternal plasma but are essential for normal fetal development.

738
01:28:36,775 --> 01:28:43,100
Insulin, steroid hormones, and thyroid hormones cross the placenta, but very slowly.

739
01:28:43,100 --> 01:28:51,325
The hormones synthesized in situ in the syncytiotrophoblast enter both the maternal and fetal circulations, but not equally .

740
01:28:51,325 --> 01:28:59,200
Examples are hCG and hPL concentrations, which are much lower in fetal plasma than in maternal plasma.

741
01:28:59,200 --> 01:29:05,900
High-molecular-weight substances usually do not traverse the placenta, but there are important exceptions.

742
01:29:05,900 --> 01:29:16,563
As discussed, one is IgG—molecular weight 160,000 Da—which is transferred by way of a specific trophoblast receptor–mediated mechanism .

743
01:29:16,563 --> 01:29:26,563
Although simple diffusion is an important method of placental transfer, the trophoblast and chorionic villus unit demonstrate enormous selectivity in transfer.

744
01:29:26,563 --> 01:29:36,300
As discussed, important in this regard is DNA methylation of transporter genes (Kerr, 2018; Simner, 2017).

745
01:29:36,300 --> 01:29:41,675
The net results are different metabolite concentrations on the two sides of the villus.

746
01:29:41,675 --> 01:29:47,700
Transfer of Oxygen and Carbon Dioxide Placental oxygen transfer is blood flow limited.

747
01:29:47,700 --> 01:30:00,213
Using estimated uteroplacental blood flow, Longo (1991) calculated oxygen delivery to be approximately 8 mL O2/min/kg of fetal weight.

748
01:30:00,213 --> 01:30:10,150
Because of the continuous passage of oxygen from maternal blood in the intervillous space to the fetus, its oxygen saturation resembles that in maternal capillaries.

749
01:30:10,150 --> 01:30:22,150
The average oxygen saturation of intervillous blood is estimated to be 65 to 75 percent, with a partial pressure (Pao2) of 30 to 35 mm Hg.

750
01:30:22,150 --> 01:30:29,025
The oxygen saturation of umbilical vein blood is similar but has a somewhat lower oxygen partial pressure .

751
01:30:29,025 --> 01:30:35,300
As noted earlier, fetal hemoglobin has a higher oxygen affinity than adult hemoglobin.

752
01:30:35,300 --> 01:30:43,338
The placenta is highly permeable to CO2, which traverses the chorionic villus by diffusion more rapidly than oxygen.

753
01:30:43,338 --> 01:30:57,188
Near term, the partial pressure of carbon dioxide (Paco2) in the umbilical arteries averages 50 mm Hg, which is approximately 5 mm Hg higher than in the maternal intervillous blood.

754
